WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.440
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4536.

2
00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:02.880
Begin Session II.]

3
00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:03.360
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alright.

4
00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.720
Today is November the first, actually, 2015.

5
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.160
It’s a Sunday.

6
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:16.320
I’m here . . . I’m Chelsea Arseneault with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU Libraries.

7
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:22.560
I’m here today with Mr. Kenneth Lee at his home in . . . it’s Westlake.

8
00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.000
<v ARTHUR KENNETH LEE>Moss Bluff.

9
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:24.816
<v ARSENEAULT>Moss Bluff.

10
00:00:24.816 --> 00:00:25.632
I’m sorry.

11
00:00:25.632 --> 00:00:26.448
Moss Bluff.

12
00:00:26.448 --> 00:00:31.344
This is our second interview together, his third interview for this project.

13
00:00:31.344 --> 00:00:37.872
We’re here to talk today a little . . . a couple follow-up questions about your memories of Mossville.

14
00:00:37.872 --> 00:00:43.992
Just going, what we talked about last time, I wanted to get some clarification questions.

15
00:00:43.992 --> 00:00:48.072
You said you were on the water board with Mossville.

16
00:00:48.072 --> 00:00:51.336
Do you remember what date Mossville got water?

17
00:00:51.336 --> 00:00:57.048
<v LEE>Exactly, no. But it was in the ‘70s, early ‘70s, that water was established.

18
00:00:57.048 --> 00:01:07.656
When the first, one of the first people to retire was Mr. Audrey Prater, I took his place on the water board, and fulfilled his term.

19
00:01:07.656 --> 00:01:10.104
And then I served a term.

20
00:01:10.104 --> 00:01:15.000
I was on the board until I left the Mossville area.

21
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:21.629
<v ARSENEAULT>So it was a , you had to apply for a federal grant?

22
00:01:21.629 --> 00:01:29.914
<v LEE>Oh, they had already done that prior to me coming aboard, on the water board.

23
00:01:29.914 --> 00:01:36.543
We got a grant from the government and we dug the well.

24
00:01:36.543 --> 00:01:38.752
It was in process.

25
00:01:38.752 --> 00:01:44.829
They hadn’t completed it, really, and that was serving the people.

26
00:01:44.829 --> 00:01:56.429
Mr. Prater decided he didn’t want to serve anymore because of his health, and so they asked me to replace him.

27
00:01:56.429 --> 00:02:03.057
And then when the real appointment came up, I stayed with them.

28
00:02:03.057 --> 00:02:05.819
I stayed with the board.

29
00:02:05.819 --> 00:02:12.448
James Rigmaiden was the president of the water board at that time.

30
00:02:12.448 --> 00:02:21.286
If I remember exactly, it was five people, or four or five people on the board.

31
00:02:21.286 --> 00:02:28.467
It served a purpose, and we got it extended a couple of times,

32
00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:40.067
and got everybody on board with the wells close out the regular old water wells, and got on the water system.

33
00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:42.829
Because of the health situation.

34
00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:49.457
Because a lot of people had the shallow wells in that area.

35
00:02:49.457 --> 00:02:59.952
It was a concern with the government about the welfare of the people in reference to the shallow wells.

36
00:02:59.952 --> 00:03:03.267
And then later the sewage came.

37
00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:06.581
It was a thriving little community.

38
00:03:06.581 --> 00:03:11.000
A lot of people really did good.

39
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:17.034
We had a kind of a flight from the area for jobs and stuff.

40
00:03:17.034 --> 00:03:25.653
Some of the people that really could have contributed to the community left the community because of the job situation.

41
00:03:25.653 --> 00:03:34.273
We had like a, I call it a brain drain during that time, during the time that I stayed mostly,

42
00:03:34.273 --> 00:03:44.185
other than being, going away to the military—maybe I discussed this before— and working in Houston [Texas], summer jobs, and stuff like that.

43
00:03:44.185 --> 00:03:49.357
I basically, most of my time I was in the Mossville area.

44
00:03:49.357 --> 00:03:58.838
A lot of my friends, like Mr. Jackson, he had to go to Houston to get a good job, good paying job.

45
00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:00.131
And brain drain.

46
00:04:00.131 --> 00:04:04.872
We had a lot of good people that really did good.

47
00:04:04.872 --> 00:04:12.199
We had some doctors and nurses, and in all walks of life, all different kind of professions.

48
00:04:12.199 --> 00:04:14.785
But most of them went away.

49
00:04:14.785 --> 00:04:21.249
And a lot of them would come back, like the Edwards girls and different ones.

50
00:04:21.249 --> 00:04:25.990
After they would go away and work, they would come back.

51
00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:36.764
Some of them came back and went to, like some of my classmates, they went away and worked for the government up in Washington, DC.

52
00:04:36.764 --> 00:04:40.212
And then when they retired, they came home.

53
00:04:40.212 --> 00:04:47.108
But they moved to Lake Charles [Louisiana] because of the expansion of the plants and stuff,

54
00:04:47.108 --> 00:04:51.848
and they were concerned about this and that being a problem.

55
00:04:51.848 --> 00:04:54.865
But to me, it was a blessing.

56
00:04:54.865 --> 00:05:00.468
To get the buyout was really a blessing, the way I seen it.

57
00:05:00.468 --> 00:05:07.364
But like I tell you, what’s good for me might not have been good for them.

58
00:05:07.364 --> 00:05:11.242
And what’s good for them wasn’t good for me.

59
00:05:11.242 --> 00:05:15.983
A lot of people having a hard time dealing with it.

60
00:05:15.983 --> 00:05:19.000
But it’s a blessing in disguise.

61
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.122
<v ARSENEAULT>How was it a blessing for you?

62
00:05:22.122 --> 00:05:30.151
<v LEE>Well, to get out of the district, to get out of there, and upgrading my standards of living.

63
00:05:30.151 --> 00:05:34.165
I had a nice house over there and everything.

64
00:05:34.165 --> 00:05:38.180
But I didn’t feel free like for my kids.

65
00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:43.532
And they were talking about the contamination this and the contamination that.

66
00:05:43.532 --> 00:05:49.777
But we all worked in the plants and worked around it, so and here,

67
00:05:49.777 --> 00:05:55.576
they gave you a chance to get out and upgrade and get bigger.

68
00:05:55.576 --> 00:06:00.036
My house is no bigger, but I got more land.

69
00:06:00.036 --> 00:06:08.957
I had already 4,000 square foot home over there in Mossville, and swimming pool, and barn, and horses and everything.

70
00:06:08.957 --> 00:06:11.633
But I only had six acres.

71
00:06:11.633 --> 00:06:13.417
Here, I got ten.

72
00:06:13.417 --> 00:06:18.324
It’s more modern than my house would have been at Mossville.

73
00:06:18.324 --> 00:06:21.446
And really, I call it a blessing.

74
00:06:21.446 --> 00:06:25.014
That’s one of my favorite quotes right there.

75
00:06:25.014 --> 00:06:32.597
<v ARSENEAULT>The will of God will not take you, or the grace of God will not protect you.

76
00:06:32.597 --> 00:06:33.043
<v LEE>Yeah.

77
00:06:33.043 --> 00:06:38.842
And I think that was one of the main things that I contributed.

78
00:06:38.842 --> 00:06:44.640
A lot of us ain’t got enough sense to get out of here.

79
00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:49.101
But hey, God going to take us out of there.

80
00:06:49.101 --> 00:06:52.223
He blessed us in a way indirectly.

81
00:06:52.223 --> 00:06:53.561
Here’s your chance.

82
00:06:53.561 --> 00:06:58.468
Your blessings come to you in all different kind of ways.

83
00:06:58.468 --> 00:07:08.727
It’s just up to you be able to accept them, and go ahead on with them and make something good out of it.

84
00:07:08.727 --> 00:07:14.079
And I think this is, that Sasol and its buyout is good.

85
00:07:14.079 --> 00:07:23.000
Because I’ve been through first one with PPG that we had property down in Bayou d’Inde and we sold.

86
00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:27.321
<v ARSENEAULT>That was the first company that started the buyouts?

87
00:07:27.321 --> 00:07:30.202
<v LEE>With me, and with my family.

88
00:07:30.202 --> 00:07:30.682
Okay?

89
00:07:30.682 --> 00:07:36.923
Then with the Lee family was down on the bayou, south of Mossville.

90
00:07:36.923 --> 00:07:44.125
There on the south side of Highway 90, we had a 200-acre strip in there.

91
00:07:44.125 --> 00:07:50.366
In the beginning, my grandfather inherited it from his dad and his mother.

92
00:07:50.366 --> 00:07:54.207
Some [industry, support industry?] bought portions of it.

93
00:07:54.207 --> 00:07:58.048
And PPG, Jupiter Chemical, bought some of it.

94
00:07:58.048 --> 00:08:02.369
It enabled me to build my house in Mossville.

95
00:08:02.369 --> 00:08:07.170
And then from there, I called it a blessing then.

96
00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:11.011
From there, Conoco bought my house in Mossville.

97
00:08:11.011 --> 00:08:11.491
Okay?

98
00:08:11.491 --> 00:08:13.412
A blessing, you understand?

99
00:08:13.412 --> 00:08:14.852
Bought my land.

100
00:08:14.852 --> 00:08:26.375
And then my mother died and we had some property, me and my brother had some property together in Mossville by my mother’s house.

101
00:08:26.375 --> 00:08:33.097
And then Sasol bought that, me and my brother’s land, and my mother’s land.

102
00:08:33.097 --> 00:08:33.577
Okay?

103
00:08:33.577 --> 00:08:34.057
Blessing.

104
00:08:34.057 --> 00:08:34.537
Okay?

105
00:08:34.537 --> 00:08:41.259
Now Sasol, my grandparents and them had land there in Mossville, and the Lemelles.

106
00:08:41.259 --> 00:08:46.060
And Sasol, we in the process of selling that now.

107
00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:47.020
Blessing again.

108
00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:49.420
You know what I’m saying?

109
00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:58.543
It’s just been, you know, I thank God every morning, because I say man, that’s five or six times.

110
00:08:58.543 --> 00:09:09.105
And then my Grandmother Lee, when we moved from Bayou d’Inde, bought some property there in Mossville and we moved the house.

111
00:09:09.105 --> 00:09:14.386
We tore the house down, but we still have the property.

112
00:09:14.386 --> 00:09:16.787
So bam, that’s another Lee.

113
00:09:16.787 --> 00:09:19.188
You know what I’m saying?

114
00:09:19.188 --> 00:09:24.469
And I say wow, I mean, if people can’t see that.

115
00:09:24.469 --> 00:09:27.349
They say, “Well, we’re not you.”

116
00:09:27.349 --> 00:09:34.551
Well, I guess not, but you’re still getting some kind of blessing out of it.

117
00:09:34.551 --> 00:09:38.872
You’re getting a chance to get out of there.

118
00:09:38.872 --> 00:09:45.594
You all been hollering about moving and this and that, and this and that,

119
00:09:45.594 --> 00:09:51.835
and they’re giving you three and four times the value of your property.

120
00:09:51.835 --> 00:09:53.276
That’s a blessing.

121
00:09:53.276 --> 00:09:57.597
<v ARSENEAULT>Why are some people not seeing it like that?

122
00:09:57.597 --> 00:10:10.080
<v LEE>I think really, the real situation is that a lot of them got tied up with these federal grants behind this hurricane when they came through.

123
00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:12.000
And they had . . .

124
00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:12.933
<v ARSENEAULT>Hurricane Katrina?

125
00:10:12.933 --> 00:10:13.399
<v LEE>Katrina.

126
00:10:13.399 --> 00:10:19.929
And some of them got that money and they put it in their house.

127
00:10:19.929 --> 00:10:31.588
They hadn’t fulfilled all the obligations to pay it off, or some stipulations in that they can’t sell it until such and such a day,

128
00:10:31.588 --> 00:10:42.782
or they get, the government gets all their money back, or take their money back out of the grant, you got to repay them.

129
00:10:42.782 --> 00:10:43.714
Different things.

130
00:10:43.714 --> 00:10:51.643
And some of them has like five or six siblings and they’re living in the family house,

131
00:10:51.643 --> 00:10:58.639
and five of the six of them have left town, but one is staying there.

132
00:10:58.639 --> 00:11:03.303
And then you coming back and they wanting to sell,

133
00:11:03.303 --> 00:11:13.097
and the one that’s still here is not going to get enough money out of it to live like they’re living,

134
00:11:13.097 --> 00:11:19.160
to be able to purchase a house and don’t have no house note.

135
00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:21.492
You know what I’m saying?

136
00:11:21.492 --> 00:11:28.021
And this is one of the reasons that some of them hesitated about signing.

137
00:11:28.021 --> 00:11:46.676
Four or five of them want to sell and this one don’t want to sell because he divided it up 150 to 200,000 dollars six ways is not enough to put you living where you don’t have a light bill,

138
00:11:46.676 --> 00:11:49.941
unless you go in the projects somewhere.

139
00:11:49.941 --> 00:11:57.403
Then if you got that eighty, $90,000, $50,000, $60,000, you can’t go live in the projects.

140
00:11:57.403 --> 00:12:03.000
And it’s kind of putting some of them in a bind.

141
00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:12.516
To my knowledge, maybe I’m incorrect on thinking, but this is something that people have told me, reason for why they can’t,

142
00:12:12.516 --> 00:12:20.734
they hesitated about selling, because they’re not going to be able to move and don’t have a house note.

143
00:12:20.734 --> 00:12:27.221
But yet it’s going to give them a break to up their standard their living.

144
00:12:27.221 --> 00:12:31.114
And some of them are on a fixed income.

145
00:12:31.114 --> 00:12:37.602
Well the ones that’s working, that’s got this five or six, seven, they don’t mind,

146
00:12:37.602 --> 00:12:46.253
because they’re going to get a chance to move and fifty, $60,000 is a good down payment on a house.

147
00:12:46.253 --> 00:12:53.173
And it’s just different situations that brings about don’t want to sell and want to sell.

148
00:12:53.173 --> 00:12:56.633
To me, it’s almost you’ve got to sell.

149
00:12:56.633 --> 00:13:02.256
Because they’ve got so much work going into vibrations and this and that.

150
00:13:02.256 --> 00:13:08.311
And the pipes and stuff, the foundations of the houses is shifting and moving.

151
00:13:08.311 --> 00:13:14.367
You guys is feeling all that work going on around there in that area.

152
00:13:14.367 --> 00:13:17.394
All of them trucks ripping and running.

153
00:13:17.394 --> 00:13:20.422
You get sick, you can’t get out.

154
00:13:20.422 --> 00:13:22.585
You know what I’m saying?

155
00:13:22.585 --> 00:13:24.315
Or if something happened.

156
00:13:24.315 --> 00:13:34.263
And this going to go on just not no three or four weeks; this going to go on for a couple of years.

157
00:13:34.263 --> 00:13:39.886
They got the majority of the people there that’s still there, elderly people.

158
00:13:39.886 --> 00:13:46.806
And by the time the ambulance get in there to get you, you can kiss it.

159
00:13:46.806 --> 00:13:58.484
That’s another thing that they have to take under consideration that it’s going to have to become almost a gated community because of the terrorists and everything.

160
00:13:58.484 --> 00:14:02.810
That they’ve got to have tough security in that area.

161
00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:08.000
Some of those roads they’re going to have to block off.

162
00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:12.375
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you mean, terrorists?

163
00:14:12.375 --> 00:14:16.750
<v LEE>Well, like this ISIS thing , , ,

164
00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:19.375
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean oil?

165
00:14:19.375 --> 00:14:20.250
<v LEE>Yeah.

166
00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:22.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

167
00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:30.795
<v LEE>Because of the process and the danger with them trying to . . . it’s kind of farfetched.

168
00:14:30.795 --> 00:14:40.762
But hey, man, nothing today . . . I could see anything trying to happen, or happening, with these people.

169
00:14:40.762 --> 00:14:53.074
That would be a great target, if they hit one of those industries here, because it would be a chain reaction.

170
00:14:53.074 --> 00:15:01.869
The government, or whatever you call it, security, it’s going to have to tighten up,

171
00:15:01.869 --> 00:15:10.664
and if you, in there, close by, we’re not really safe where we at here.

172
00:15:10.664 --> 00:15:17.699
But we’re much safer than we were in Mossville . . . from the leaks,

173
00:15:17.699 --> 00:15:29.426
and because there’ve been times that we had leaks and we had to stay in and the alarm went off,

174
00:15:29.426 --> 00:15:34.702
and you had to stay . . . what they call that?

175
00:15:34.702 --> 00:15:35.875
Shut in?

176
00:15:35.875 --> 00:15:43.497
Where you had to stay in your house and turn off all your . . .

177
00:15:43.497 --> 00:15:45.842
<v ARSENEAULT>What plant was leaking?

178
00:15:45.842 --> 00:15:47.601
<v LEE>Vista, Georgia Gulf.

179
00:15:47.601 --> 00:15:48.188
Explosions.

180
00:15:48.188 --> 00:15:53.464
And I think PPG a couple of times, and . . .

181
00:15:53.464 --> 00:15:55.810
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it ever Axiall?

182
00:15:55.810 --> 00:16:03.432
<v LEE>Yeah, well, PPG, that’s the old Axiall, it was PPG at that time.

183
00:16:03.432 --> 00:16:15.158
And since Axiall have had it, there have been a couple of incidents where they had a shelter in place.

184
00:16:15.158 --> 00:16:28.643
Because they had the big speakers throughout the community down there, and they would come on when they had something in the area.

185
00:16:28.643 --> 00:16:38.610
Conoco, Olin, or some of them would have a chemical leak, a train car derailment, or something.

186
00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:43.301
They had a warning system, and it helped.

187
00:16:43.301 --> 00:16:55.027
But it was mostly because you, most of the wind comes out of the south and the southwest and everything.

188
00:16:55.027 --> 00:17:06.753
And the way Mossville was situated there, it was kind of fully almost surrounded by industry, some type of industry.

189
00:17:06.753 --> 00:17:19.652
Not all chemical, because the electrical thing was turning off, and Citgo and Firestone, PPG, and all of that to the south.

190
00:17:19.652 --> 00:17:27.274
To the east you had Conoco and Georgia Gulf and Sasol was Vista.

191
00:17:27.274 --> 00:17:30.792
It was pretty well locked in.

192
00:17:30.792 --> 00:17:37.241
It gave me a chance to get out of the boat.

193
00:17:37.241 --> 00:17:39.000
You understand?

194
00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:46.044
My grandfather used to say, “You’ve got too much in the boat, sooner or later it’s going to run over.

195
00:17:46.044 --> 00:17:48.157
You’re going to have some spills.”

196
00:17:48.157 --> 00:17:53.440
So if you get a chance to get out of there, get out of there.

197
00:17:53.440 --> 00:17:55.201
You know what I’m saying?

198
00:17:55.201 --> 00:18:00.132
We got in and by then, they wanting to buy, it’s time to go.

199
00:18:00.132 --> 00:18:04.358
But he died before, he was in the process of selling it.

200
00:18:04.358 --> 00:18:06.824
But he died before everything went through.

201
00:18:06.824 --> 00:18:14.572
And then by the time they did all the paperwork and everything, my dad was in charge, he had a heart attack.

202
00:18:14.572 --> 00:18:16.333
So my brother said, “Kenny.”

203
00:18:16.333 --> 00:18:17.742
I say, “What, Rick?”

204
00:18:17.742 --> 00:18:22.673
He say, “Man, I want to sell it, but it’s a bad thing here.

205
00:18:22.673 --> 00:18:28.660
Everybody, when it gets time, before you get the money, everybody, we had two in a row.”

206
00:18:28.660 --> 00:18:31.126
He said, “We going to be three.”

207
00:18:31.126 --> 00:18:33.591
I said, “Hell, we’re going to try.”

208
00:18:33.591 --> 00:18:35.352
You know, what Grandpa said?

209
00:18:35.352 --> 00:18:37.818
He said, “The bowl running over, son.

210
00:18:37.818 --> 00:18:43.453
They going to have us competing around it.” [Lee clarifies competing to get out of it].

211
00:18:43.453 --> 00:18:45.918
He said, “It’s time to get out.

212
00:18:45.918 --> 00:18:51.906
If you get a chance to get out of the bowl, they going to treat you fair.

213
00:18:51.906 --> 00:18:54.019
Go ahead on and get out.”

214
00:18:54.019 --> 00:19:01.063
Because he had some wisdom about it, some things he would tell you that it wouldn’t register at the time.

215
00:19:01.063 --> 00:19:05.994
Like this thing about, maybe I told you before, about educating you about ignorance.

216
00:19:05.994 --> 00:19:08.811
And it worked the hell out of you.

217
00:19:08.811 --> 00:19:14.799
And people down the street were doing something, and my dad, he’d make us go help them.

218
00:19:14.799 --> 00:19:18.673
We said, “Daddy, why do we got to go help them?”

219
00:19:18.673 --> 00:19:20.434
“I’m educating you about ignorance.

220
00:19:20.434 --> 00:19:26.421
You’re going to have to work with that [shit?] or get you a good education,” he said.

221
00:19:26.421 --> 00:19:31.000
“And the education is the key to get you out of here.”

222
00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:35.123
<v ARSENEAULT>So what would happen when the alarms went off?

223
00:19:35.123 --> 00:19:38.330
<v LEE>Well, they would stay shelter in place.

224
00:19:38.330 --> 00:19:43.828
A lot of times they shut all the traffic down coming through.

225
00:19:43.828 --> 00:19:50.241
Just depending on what kind of spill or release they had—how long or what.

226
00:19:50.241 --> 00:19:53.906
And there’s times they shut the interstate down.

227
00:19:53.906 --> 00:19:59.862
Depending on which way the wind’s blowing, too, they shut the interstate down,

228
00:19:59.862 --> 00:20:09.941
or you had to stay in your house and turn off all your air condition and stuff because of the suction of,

229
00:20:09.941 --> 00:20:14.064
and a lot of people got sick over it.

230
00:20:14.064 --> 00:20:17.271
You got caught in the wrong place.

231
00:20:17.271 --> 00:20:27.808
And if you in transit there and you’re passing by there and there’s chlorine, formalin or whatever the leak was, it get you.

232
00:20:27.808 --> 00:20:32.847
A lot of people had to go to the emergency rooms.

233
00:20:32.847 --> 00:20:36.970
But like they say, “Oh, it’s not that bad.”

234
00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:42.926
But if it’s bad enough to shut the interstate down, like he said.

235
00:20:42.926 --> 00:20:48.882
Grandfather used to say, “Too much of anything that kills will kill you.”

236
00:20:48.882 --> 00:20:56.212
If it kills something, too much of it will kill you. [Portion omitted per Lee’s request]

237
00:20:56.212 --> 00:20:59.419
<v ARSENEAULT>You were in college at the time?

238
00:20:59.419 --> 00:21:03.084
<v LEE>Yeah, playing first base at Grambling [State University].

239
00:21:03.084 --> 00:21:04.000
Scholarship.

240
00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:06.772
<v ARSENEAULT>So you remember Coach Eddie Robinson.

241
00:21:06.772 --> 00:21:10.469
<v LEE>Yeah, I know . . . I knew Coach Rob, yeah.

242
00:21:10.469 --> 00:21:13.241
My brother played football up there.

243
00:21:13.241 --> 00:21:16.938
He was a four-year captain under Eddie Robinson.

244
00:21:16.938 --> 00:21:17.400
<v ARSENEAULT>What?!

245
00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:17.862
<v LEE>Yeah!

246
00:21:17.862 --> 00:21:25.255
We went up there this year and my granddaughter, the granddaughter’s a cheerleader up there now.

247
00:21:25.255 --> 00:21:29.876
And my brother’s in the hall of fame up there.

248
00:21:29.876 --> 00:21:32.648
I knew Coach Rob real well.

249
00:21:32.648 --> 00:21:35.883
Coach Holly, Coach Rob during the ‘60s.

250
00:21:35.883 --> 00:21:39.117
I was always . . . I had no name.

251
00:21:39.117 --> 00:21:43.276
I was always related to, as Richard’s little brother.

252
00:21:43.276 --> 00:21:46.972
My brother was a big man on campus.

253
00:21:46.972 --> 00:21:52.055
In fact, he played for the Boston Patriots and the 49ers.

254
00:21:52.055 --> 00:21:55.752
I was up there during the Grambling heydays.

255
00:21:55.752 --> 00:21:57.600
Best of the best.

256
00:21:57.600 --> 00:22:04.531
Every year we have a gathering, a reunion of, call them the G-Men, Rob’s boys.

257
00:22:04.531 --> 00:22:07.303
We all played. . . ARSENEAULT: Coach Robinson?

258
00:22:07.303 --> 00:22:09.152
They called him Rob?

259
00:22:09.152 --> 00:22:11.000
<v LEE>Rob Boys, yeah.

260
00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:12.104
<v ARSENEAULT>Rob Boys.

261
00:22:12.104 --> 00:22:12.656
<v LEE>Yeah.

262
00:22:12.656 --> 00:22:18.176
And the G-Men, the Richard Lee gathering, the G Men.

263
00:22:18.176 --> 00:22:23.144
And he, my brother’s the one that started it.

264
00:22:23.144 --> 00:22:31.976
Every year around the nineteenth of June, the weekend before, the weekend after, we get together.

265
00:22:31.976 --> 00:22:44.120
Seventy-five, eighty, 150 of us get together and relive and go over some of the things and have some speakers come in.

266
00:22:44.120 --> 00:22:51.848
And Coach [Broderick] Fobbs spoke this year, the new coach, football coach, he spoke.

267
00:22:51.848 --> 00:22:56.264
In fact, his wife is from Lake Charles.

268
00:22:56.264 --> 00:23:02.888
We went up there to one of the home games this year.

269
00:23:02.888 --> 00:23:10.616
We went around my old baseball coach, he was the assistant coach back then.

270
00:23:10.616 --> 00:23:11.720
He’s there.

271
00:23:11.720 --> 00:23:20.000
We all went by the athletic building and talked and relived the old times.

272
00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:22.313
<v ARSENEAULT>What year did you go?

273
00:23:22.313 --> 00:23:24.626
What year did you start?

274
00:23:24.626 --> 00:23:27.865
<v LEE>I graduated from high school in ‘66.

275
00:23:27.865 --> 00:23:29.715
I went to Grambling.

276
00:23:29.715 --> 00:23:37.117
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you live, you would have to have lived there, because it’s like three hours away.

277
00:23:37.117 --> 00:23:39.893
<v LEE>Yeah, I lived there on campus.

278
00:23:39.893 --> 00:23:41.281
<v ARSENEAULT>In a dorm?

279
00:23:41.281 --> 00:23:41.744
<v LEE>Yeah.

280
00:23:41.744 --> 00:23:45.907
And then I had an apartment off campus, too.

281
00:23:45.907 --> 00:23:55.160
I had a homeboy, Prater, was already up there at Grambling and he had a room, a house off campus.

282
00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:58.399
I kind of stayed between the two.

283
00:23:58.399 --> 00:24:02.562
You know how it is back then, you’re eighteen,

284
00:24:02.562 --> 00:24:09.502
nineteen years old and your homeboy . . . And we were close before he went up there.

285
00:24:09.502 --> 00:24:16.441
So he had some space in the apartment, in the house where he was living.

286
00:24:16.441 --> 00:24:19.680
So I kind of lived with him.

287
00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:25.231
I had a car and I was back and forth with him.

288
00:24:25.231 --> 00:24:28.470
You know how it is in college.

289
00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:32.171
College days was great days, especially back then.

290
00:24:32.171 --> 00:24:34.484
Everybody knew everybody, just about.

291
00:24:34.484 --> 00:24:38.648
You had about four thousand kids at Grambling then.

292
00:24:38.648 --> 00:24:47.438
It wasn’t as big as it is now, but it was kind of neat that everybody knew my brother,

293
00:24:47.438 --> 00:24:52.064
because he was up there two years before I went.

294
00:24:52.064 --> 00:24:56.228
I was always related to as “Richard’s little brother.”

295
00:24:56.228 --> 00:24:57.616
“What’s your name?”

296
00:24:57.616 --> 00:25:05.480
Just like this, up there and I was walking up them stands, and it’s about 150 steps.

297
00:25:05.480 --> 00:25:08.719
And man, I say, [ . . . ?] “Hey, Little Richard!

298
00:25:08.719 --> 00:25:11.032
Want me to call 9-1-1?”

299
00:25:11.032 --> 00:25:14.270
I call it the little man section.

300
00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:22.135
I say, “Hey, I’m not coming up there to the little man section and meet you all.”

301
00:25:22.135 --> 00:25:23.986
But it’s all good.

302
00:25:23.986 --> 00:25:27.224
And that was back in the day.

303
00:25:27.224 --> 00:25:30.000
But I enjoyed Grambling, man.

304
00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:40.070
Mossville remind me a lot of about it, because a lot of the kids from Mossville went to Grambling.

305
00:25:40.070 --> 00:25:48.020
A lot of my cousins and stuff went to Grambling before me and after me.

306
00:25:48.020 --> 00:25:50.140
There’s some there now.

307
00:25:50.140 --> 00:25:53.320
My daughter, my granddaughter is there.

308
00:25:53.320 --> 00:26:02.860
My son went to Grambling, and he went to Mississippi Junior College, and then he transferred to Grambling.

309
00:26:02.860 --> 00:26:07.100
His daughter, in fact, that’s his daughter there.

310
00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:14.520
And my sister-in-law, my brother’s wife went to Grambling, my niece went to Grambling.

311
00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:23.000
And a bunch of the Prater girls from Mossville went to Grambling, which are my relatives.

312
00:26:23.000 --> 00:26:27.240
You have a little Mossville community within Grambling.

313
00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:34.660
You have a problem with something, they kind of looked out for one another.

314
00:26:34.660 --> 00:26:41.550
And they kind of recruited, because Johnny Comeaux, he helped recruit my brother.

315
00:26:41.550 --> 00:26:49.500
Johnny was up there, athlete from Mossville, first athlete from Mossville, to go to Grambling.

316
00:26:49.500 --> 00:26:54.270
He was a high school all American in basketball.

317
00:26:54.270 --> 00:27:01.160
In fact, he played for New Orleans [Louisiana] and several other teams there.

318
00:27:01.160 --> 00:27:05.400
I think, that was in the early ‘60s.

319
00:27:05.400 --> 00:27:12.290
And he recruited my brother, helped recruit my brother to come to Grambling.

320
00:27:12.290 --> 00:27:16.000
And the Prater girls were there.

321
00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:24.800
But speaking of the partying and stuff at Mossville at the Paradise Club, that was a special thing.

322
00:27:24.800 --> 00:27:29.689
And I got a chance to see those people there.

323
00:27:29.689 --> 00:27:37.022
And went horseback riding with Bobby Blue Bland and different ones like that, different things.

324
00:27:37.022 --> 00:27:45.822
And Mr. Montgomery and them would cook for them during . . . a lot of times they would be playing,

325
00:27:45.822 --> 00:27:53.156
like on a Friday or Saturday, they’d get there on the Wednesday or the Tuesday.

326
00:27:53.156 --> 00:27:56.089
Because they was making a tour.

327
00:27:56.089 --> 00:28:00.000
And they called it the Chitlin Circuit.

328
00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:00.509
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

329
00:28:00.509 --> 00:28:02.035
What is that?

330
00:28:02.035 --> 00:28:04.580
What is the Chitlin Circuit?

331
00:28:04.580 --> 00:28:14.248
<v LEE>That was kind of a name they used for the black circuit that the bands would travel around on.

332
00:28:14.248 --> 00:28:19.846
And I don't know where the name from, who started it.

333
00:28:19.846 --> 00:28:31.549
But they would call it the Chitlin Circuit, referring to blacks, I guess, because a lot of blacks liked the chitlins and stuff.

334
00:28:31.549 --> 00:28:42.744
They would cook, and barbecue, and boil crawfish, depending upon what time of the year that it was that they were here.

335
00:28:42.744 --> 00:28:46.306
They’d spend two or three days here.

336
00:28:46.306 --> 00:28:56.484
A lot of them didn’t, would come in the community and be there at the Paradise and they would practice.

337
00:28:56.484 --> 00:29:01.572
You’d get a chance to see them practice and singing.

338
00:29:01.572 --> 00:29:09.205
Some of us worked in there before, you know, wait tables and stuff like that.

339
00:29:09.205 --> 00:29:11.749
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was the Paradise Club?

340
00:29:11.749 --> 00:29:13.276
<v LEE>On Prater Road.

341
00:29:13.276 --> 00:29:14.294
Prater Road.

342
00:29:14.294 --> 00:29:21.927
It was, in fact, it was on the same street as I was raised on,

343
00:29:21.927 --> 00:29:32.613
and it was only a few houses down from where I lived most of my life with my mother and them.

344
00:29:32.613 --> 00:29:41.263
At one time or another, it was two or three different places we lived down Prater Road.

345
00:29:41.263 --> 00:29:46.861
And Daddy built a house, and this, that and the other.

346
00:29:46.861 --> 00:29:55.003
And then him and Mama divorced and we bought another place there and built the house.

347
00:29:55.003 --> 00:30:04.671
But anyway, the streets, Prater Road, from Highway 90 to the Old Spanish Trail, they’d be parked, people parked.

348
00:30:04.671 --> 00:30:12.304
And they letting them park in their yard for fifty cents and stuff like that.

349
00:30:12.304 --> 00:30:15.866
It was a big thing Friday night.

350
00:30:15.866 --> 00:30:20.446
Bobby Blue Bland’s going to be in the house.

351
00:30:20.446 --> 00:30:28.587
Saturday night, Bobby Blue Bland is going to be, or BB King is going to be,

352
00:30:28.587 --> 00:30:34.185
and Little Alfred’s going to open . . . Little Mack’s going to open.

353
00:30:34.185 --> 00:30:39.273
Little Shef, Sheppard’s [Mack Sheppard] going to open for him.

354
00:30:39.273 --> 00:30:48.433
You got Otis Redding, you got Joe Tex and them Barbara Lynn, a left-hand guitar player, a lady.

355
00:30:48.433 --> 00:30:59.119
And this lady that played on this story they had there about Etta James, I seen her there at the Paradise.

356
00:30:59.119 --> 00:31:03.190
Man, she could really . . . she could go, man.

357
00:31:03.190 --> 00:31:05.734
It was a real treat.

358
00:31:05.734 --> 00:31:18.965
But you look at it back then, it wasn’t no big thing because it wasn’t nothing to say, “Hey man, Joe Tex is coming to town.

359
00:31:18.965 --> 00:31:21.000
Coming to Mossville!”

360
00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:23.561
<v ARSENEAULT>How did you all find out?

361
00:31:23.561 --> 00:31:25.695
Was there like a poster?

362
00:31:25.695 --> 00:31:30.817
<v LEE>Yeah, they put posters out and it would be on the radio.

363
00:31:30.817 --> 00:31:38.500
Bubba Lutcher had a radio show on, I don't know if it was, I forget the call numbers.

364
00:31:38.500 --> 00:31:44.902
But anyway, they’d have a soul hour, like three or four hours on the radio.

365
00:31:44.902 --> 00:31:49.598
<v ARSENEAULT>Was Bubba Lutcher . . . Was there a black newspaper in Lake Charles?

366
00:31:49.598 --> 00:31:52.159
<v LEE>Not at that time there wasn’t.

367
00:31:52.159 --> 00:31:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>It was just the Lake Charles American Press?

368
00:31:56.000 --> 00:31:58.200
<v LEE>Yeah, the American Press.

369
00:31:58.200 --> 00:32:08.650
But they’d put signs everywhere, like at the little cafes and the, or little bus stops and different things.

370
00:32:08.650 --> 00:32:15.800
And Bubba Lutcher would be on the radio from like one o’clock till,

371
00:32:15.800 --> 00:32:24.050
he’d come on after the news till about three or four o’clock in the afternoon.

372
00:32:24.050 --> 00:32:26.250
Three or four hours.

373
00:32:26.250 --> 00:32:36.700
And then you had like Nashville, Tennessee, they had a radio station there that would come on at night.

374
00:32:36.700 --> 00:32:51.000
You could catch it and they mostly talked about the Chitlin Circuit, about where, who was, that’s why, I guess, we called it the Chitlin Circuit.

375
00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:54.850
Because Nashville, John R. was the deejay.

376
00:32:54.850 --> 00:32:57.600
I never forget that name.

377
00:32:57.600 --> 00:33:06.400
He would come on and tell you Ray Charles is in such and such a place,

378
00:33:06.400 --> 00:33:16.300
and Bobby Blue Bland is going to be down in Louisiana and different artists going to be where.

379
00:33:16.300 --> 00:33:24.000
It kind of kept you up with the happenings on the Chitlin Circuit.

380
00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:30.177
And then the local radio station would have soul hours, they’d call it,

381
00:33:30.177 --> 00:33:40.155
and Bubba Lutcher would talk about it and tell me, I mean, he’d tell us who and where they were playing.

382
00:33:40.155 --> 00:33:52.508
And balls at an auditorium there in Lake Charles, and between balls and the Paradise were the two spots where the big name entertainments would be.

383
00:33:52.508 --> 00:34:03.912
Everybody liked to prefer to go to the Paradise because it wasn’t in the city and you could go all night at the Paradise.

384
00:34:03.912 --> 00:34:11.514
Where at in like in the city, you had to shut it down at certain hours.

385
00:34:11.514 --> 00:34:22.442
But the Paradise, they had a little café at one time right there on the grounds that you could eat, and get food.

386
00:34:22.442 --> 00:34:25.293
It just had it going on.

387
00:34:25.293 --> 00:34:30.044
That was the, everybody wanted to go to the Paradise.

388
00:34:30.044 --> 00:34:35.746
It was an upstairs and a downstairs, had a dance floor downstairs,

389
00:34:35.746 --> 00:34:44.298
had a balcony around on both sides, and in the back they had a balcony, too, up top.

390
00:34:44.298 --> 00:34:50.000
And they’d come down there, the dance floor in the middle.

391
00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:52.048
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there a bar?

392
00:34:52.048 --> 00:34:54.608
<v LEE>Yeah, there was a bar.

393
00:34:54.608 --> 00:34:57.167
The bar was all night.

394
00:34:57.167 --> 00:35:04.335
The southwest corner of the building on the bottom floor, you had a bar.

395
00:35:04.335 --> 00:35:09.455
And they had one upstairs, too, a smaller bar upstairs.

396
00:35:09.455 --> 00:35:16.110
Because they had stairs on both sides on the west end of it.

397
00:35:16.110 --> 00:35:20.206
It was, yeah, it was nice back then.

398
00:35:20.206 --> 00:35:22.766
You know what I’m saying?

399
00:35:22.766 --> 00:35:30.445
Back in the ‘50s and ‘60s and early ‘70s, all of this was going on.

400
00:35:30.445 --> 00:35:38.124
And in the ‘50s there, we stayed about three houses down from the Paradise Club.

401
00:35:38.124 --> 00:35:43.756
And we all, you know, we was all raised like family.

402
00:35:43.756 --> 00:35:52.459
They had some, Mr. Montgomery had some boys, Alan and Nelson, we all played together coming up.

403
00:35:52.459 --> 00:35:57.579
They had their work around there, and we worked, too.

404
00:35:57.579 --> 00:36:01.675
Mr. Valery would give us a little change.

405
00:36:01.675 --> 00:36:11.914
People might order some food from the little café, hot links or something during the dance or something like that,

406
00:36:11.914 --> 00:36:18.057
and we would bring them and deliver them and stuff like that.

407
00:36:18.057 --> 00:36:24.713
And we’d clean up and put the tablecloths on the table back then.

408
00:36:24.713 --> 00:36:37.000
After the dance, we’d go all get together and he would hire us to help him clean up and park cars or whatever.

409
00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:40.233
The neighborhood kind of centered around it.

410
00:36:40.233 --> 00:36:42.080
It was super entertainment.

411
00:36:42.080 --> 00:36:53.164
Because you hear today about how the rock stars and everything, but back then, they were stars, but they were everyday people to us.

412
00:36:53.164 --> 00:36:58.705
You’d look for them, and they’d say, “We’ll see you next year.

413
00:36:58.705 --> 00:37:01.938
We’re going to do this next year.

414
00:37:01.938 --> 00:37:07.018
Make sure y’all keep them horses, we’re going to go riding.”

415
00:37:07.018 --> 00:37:12.560
Because I seen Bobby Blue Bland at Jazz Fest in New Orleans.

416
00:37:12.560 --> 00:37:20.873
I went, I told him what was going on and I said I wanted to talk with him.

417
00:37:20.873 --> 00:37:22.720
And he said, “Why?”

418
00:37:22.720 --> 00:37:29.647
He said, “Man, I used to ride horses with that man in Mossville years ago.”

419
00:37:29.647 --> 00:37:31.495
And he remembered that.

420
00:37:31.495 --> 00:37:37.960
He said that the Paradise Club, he said, “Man, them were some fun times.”

421
00:37:37.960 --> 00:37:40.269
He said, “Life was simple.”

422
00:37:40.269 --> 00:37:46.735
He said, “What’s the name of that road we used to go ride on?

423
00:37:46.735 --> 00:37:50.891
That place that was covered with trees, the brimstone.”

424
00:37:50.891 --> 00:37:54.124
I said, “That was the old Brimstone.”

425
00:37:54.124 --> 00:37:56.433
There was an old pipeline.

426
00:37:56.433 --> 00:38:02.436
It was an old brick road, but they turned it into the pipeline.

427
00:38:02.436 --> 00:38:08.902
It ran just like Old Spanish Trail did, and it ran east and west.

428
00:38:08.902 --> 00:38:11.673
We could ride horses on it.

429
00:38:11.673 --> 00:38:18.138
You could ride all the way to Sulphur on that Brimstone, the old Brimstone.

430
00:38:18.138 --> 00:38:21.371
And that’s where we would go ride.

431
00:38:21.371 --> 00:38:25.989
He told me, he talked about it and he laughed.

432
00:38:25.989 --> 00:38:32.916
He asked about Mr. Montgomery and all, everybody, how they was doing and all that.

433
00:38:32.916 --> 00:38:36.149
He said, “That was the good days.”

434
00:38:36.149 --> 00:38:41.229
He said, “We don’t have those days like that no more.”

435
00:38:41.229 --> 00:38:44.000
And I said, “You’re right.”

436
00:38:44.000 --> 00:38:56.572
Some of the younger people that were with me was related to Mr. Montgomery, the Bernard girls, Uncle John’s daughters, Gwen and Sandra and them.

437
00:38:56.572 --> 00:38:58.584
And they were surprised.

438
00:38:58.584 --> 00:39:02.607
They said, “He remember Uncle Valery and them?”

439
00:39:02.607 --> 00:39:11.156
I say, I’m telling you, it ain’t like he just came here one time or two times,

440
00:39:11.156 --> 00:39:17.191
it was kind of like a yearly, every year, just like Christmas.

441
00:39:17.191 --> 00:39:22.220
They were coming, because they made that circle, that tour.

442
00:39:22.220 --> 00:39:30.266
A lot of times they would be coming out of New Orleans or Baton Rouge [Louisiana].

443
00:39:30.266 --> 00:39:33.786
And then, Lake Charles and Lafayette [Louisiana].

444
00:39:33.786 --> 00:39:41.329
I mean, maybe Lafayette, Lake Charles, and they’d go on into Houston, making their tour.

445
00:39:41.329 --> 00:39:44.347
Man, they did that for years.

446
00:39:44.347 --> 00:39:49.879
And then when Paradise shut down, it was like a blow.

447
00:39:49.879 --> 00:39:55.410
Mr. Valery got old and the kids didn’t keep it up.

448
00:39:55.410 --> 00:40:04.462
They ran the club for a while and then just got where they changed it into a motel.

449
00:40:04.462 --> 00:40:11.000
He just, after a while, they just closed the doors on it.

450
00:40:11.000 --> 00:40:14.189
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the name of the motel?

451
00:40:14.189 --> 00:40:15.556
<v LEE>The Paradise Motel.

452
00:40:15.556 --> 00:40:16.467
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh. [laughter]

453
00:40:16.467 --> 00:40:16.923
<v LEE>Yeah.

454
00:40:16.923 --> 00:40:24.669
And then Mr. Rodney Caesar opened a little club, a small club there on the Paradise grounds.

455
00:40:24.669 --> 00:40:33.781
Where Mr. Montgomery and them used to stay years ago, before they built that big house there on Prater Road,

456
00:40:33.781 --> 00:40:39.704
they had a little small house on the front of the Paradise Club.

457
00:40:39.704 --> 00:40:42.893
They tore the Paradise down and everything.

458
00:40:42.893 --> 00:40:50.639
And Mr. Caesar, Rodney Caesar, opened a little club there on the ground for the Paradise Club.

459
00:40:50.639 --> 00:40:56.562
He took that house and converted it into a little small private club.

460
00:40:56.562 --> 00:41:01.574
It went on until he died a couple of years ago.

461
00:41:01.574 --> 00:41:05.675
He ran it for about twenty-some years for sure.

462
00:41:05.675 --> 00:41:13.420
The exact date, I couldn’t tell you, but I know he ran it for a long time.

463
00:41:13.420 --> 00:41:18.888
Because I was doing the deputy thing then, the deputy sheriff thing.

464
00:41:18.888 --> 00:41:24.355
When Mr. Caesar opened that club where the Paradise used to be.

465
00:41:24.355 --> 00:41:28.000
Are there any other questions or anything?

466
00:41:28.000 --> 00:41:29.462
<v ARSENEAULT>I wanted to know . . .

467
00:41:29.462 --> 00:41:32.019
<v LEE>I gave you a list of the . . .

468
00:41:32.019 --> 00:41:33.115
<v ARSENEAULT>The musicians, yeah.

469
00:41:33.115 --> 00:41:36.404
<v LEE>. . .the musicians that I got a chance to see.

470
00:41:36.404 --> 00:41:38.231
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have a favorite?

471
00:41:38.231 --> 00:41:38.596
<v LEE>Yeah.

472
00:41:38.596 --> 00:41:39.327
<v ARSENEAULT>Who was . . .

473
00:41:39.327 --> 00:41:40.058
<v LEE>Etta James.

474
00:41:40.058 --> 00:41:41.519
Etta James, man, she . . .

475
00:41:41.519 --> 00:41:42.615
<v ARSENEAULT>Why was that?

476
00:41:42.615 --> 00:41:44.442
Why was she your favorite?

477
00:41:44.442 --> 00:41:44.808
<v LEE>[Pardon?]

478
00:41:44.808 --> 00:41:47.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Why was she your favorite?

479
00:41:47.000 --> 00:41:57.343
<v LEE>She could sing, and the songs that she would sing, it was like things you could relate to.

480
00:41:57.343 --> 00:42:02.515
All the music I kind of relate to it.

481
00:42:02.515 --> 00:42:12.858
But Etta James, that song that she sings, “I’d Rather Go Blind than to See You Walk Away.”

482
00:42:12.858 --> 00:42:17.455
And I got it now, I play it.

483
00:42:17.455 --> 00:42:22.052
I got a CD and that’s my favorite.

484
00:42:22.052 --> 00:42:25.500
You ever seen the Cadillac movie?

485
00:42:25.500 --> 00:42:26.075
<v ARSENEAULT>No.

486
00:42:26.075 --> 00:42:30.672
<v LEE>Well she starred, I mean, what’s her name?

487
00:42:30.672 --> 00:42:31.246
Beyoncé.

488
00:42:31.246 --> 00:42:32.396
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, yeah.

489
00:42:32.396 --> 00:42:33.545
Yeah, yeah.

490
00:42:33.545 --> 00:42:36.418
<v LEE>Plays Etta James in that.

491
00:42:36.418 --> 00:42:38.716
<v ARSENEAULT>I think I, yeah.

492
00:42:38.716 --> 00:42:39.291
Yeah.

493
00:42:39.291 --> 00:42:41.015
<v LEE>She could sing.

494
00:42:41.015 --> 00:42:48.485
Man, she said she’d rather go blind than to see you walk away.

495
00:42:48.485 --> 00:42:51.358
And that’s been my favorite.

496
00:42:51.358 --> 00:42:54.806
I play that all the time.

497
00:42:54.806 --> 00:43:04.000
I have two or three other CDs that I play, but that’s my favorite song.

498
00:43:04.000 --> 00:43:08.302
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about the Montgomerys?

499
00:43:08.302 --> 00:43:11.990
<v LEE>They were some real Creole people.

500
00:43:11.990 --> 00:43:19.365
Mr. Valery never met a stranger, I don’t believe, in his life.

501
00:43:19.365 --> 00:43:25.510
And they would cook and, “Hey, you all come eat!”

502
00:43:25.510 --> 00:43:29.198
They were real open-hearted and kind.

503
00:43:29.198 --> 00:43:35.958
Valery, Mr. Montgomery, he was a real asset to the community.

504
00:43:35.958 --> 00:43:45.792
“You all going to New Iberia play ball, here coach,” he had something for the boys.

505
00:43:45.792 --> 00:43:48.865
You know what I’m saying?

506
00:43:48.865 --> 00:43:52.552
Help the team [Wes?] or something.

507
00:43:52.552 --> 00:43:59.927
Or they got some kind of activity for the school or something.

508
00:43:59.927 --> 00:44:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>He would give money?

509
00:44:03.000 --> 00:44:06.051
<v LEE>He’d give money, contribute to it.

510
00:44:06.051 --> 00:44:14.696
That helped, indirectly helped everybody in the community, because a lot of times we would go places,

511
00:44:14.696 --> 00:44:27.410
like normal things like go on the bus, but we had to go in cars because we didn’t have the facilities and everything to go.

512
00:44:27.410 --> 00:44:37.072
But people in the neighborhood, like Mr. Montgomery and Wes Montgomery and Valery, [Oreece?] Thomas, and people like that.

513
00:44:37.072 --> 00:44:42.157
Mr. Josh Rigmaiden and all of them, they would contribute.

514
00:44:42.157 --> 00:44:46.225
“Boy, I know you ain’t got no money.

515
00:44:46.225 --> 00:44:52.836
Here’s a couple of dollars when you all get over there to spend.”

516
00:44:52.836 --> 00:44:56.904
Things like that, that meant a lot then.

517
00:44:56.904 --> 00:45:04.024
“I’m going to cook a dinner for you, because you all won these games.”

518
00:45:04.024 --> 00:45:05.549
Stuff like that.

519
00:45:05.549 --> 00:45:07.584
It meant a lot.

520
00:45:07.584 --> 00:45:11.652
And if you had some kind of problems,

521
00:45:11.652 --> 00:45:21.823
you could go to them and they knew the right people to go see to try to get it corrected.

522
00:45:21.823 --> 00:45:29.959
Some of the boys got in trouble or something, they would kind of talk to them.

523
00:45:29.959 --> 00:45:35.044
They was kind of some role models in the community.

524
00:45:35.044 --> 00:45:38.604
They would help anybody that they could.

525
00:45:38.604 --> 00:45:44.198
A lot of people stayed at, with Mr. Valery and them.

526
00:45:44.198 --> 00:45:53.352
They’d come to town, wouldn’t have a place to stay or something like that, he’d take them in.

527
00:45:53.352 --> 00:46:03.014
He had a couple of rent houses back then and they’d stay there and they’d get on their feet.

528
00:46:03.014 --> 00:46:13.184
He just was the kind of a man that you was proud to know and to be associated with him.

529
00:46:13.184 --> 00:46:18.270
I’d say, to kind of, how you would say that?

530
00:46:18.270 --> 00:46:22.338
He was just an asset to the community.

531
00:46:22.338 --> 00:46:32.000
I just can’t put it no better than that, I guess, because he helped a lot of people.

532
00:46:32.000 --> 00:46:35.735
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he ever get involved in politics?

533
00:46:35.735 --> 00:46:36.269
<v LEE>Yeah.

534
00:46:36.269 --> 00:46:36.803
Yeah.

535
00:46:36.803 --> 00:46:37.336
Yeah.

536
00:46:37.336 --> 00:46:47.475
In fact, the first time I saw or met a governor, I met the governor at Mr. Montgomery’s house.

537
00:46:47.475 --> 00:46:49.610
<v ARSENEAULT>What governor was it?

538
00:46:49.610 --> 00:46:50.677
<v LEE>Edwin Edwards.

539
00:46:50.677 --> 00:46:54.946
He was a good friend to Edwin Edwards.

540
00:46:54.946 --> 00:47:00.816
That was the first time I met a governor in person.

541
00:47:00.816 --> 00:47:04.552
And I met him at Mr. Valery’s.

542
00:47:04.552 --> 00:47:15.224
The sheriff Ham Reed back in the day and a lot of people, lots of people, a lot of politicians.

543
00:47:15.224 --> 00:47:23.762
He was influencing a lot of the community politics, and police juries and stuff like that.

544
00:47:23.762 --> 00:47:32.834
He would always give some kind of function for that, for the political movements in the community.

545
00:47:32.834 --> 00:47:40.305
Just, he was, I’ll say it again, he was an asset to the community.

546
00:47:40.305 --> 00:47:42.439
Not only in politics.

547
00:47:42.439 --> 00:47:47.242
Just structure, anything that he could help somebody with.

548
00:47:47.242 --> 00:47:55.247
He wasn’t . . . And I’ve never heard him badmouth nobody, even though he was in politics.

549
00:47:55.247 --> 00:48:06.987
It was like your judgment is your judgment, and what you do, we’re not going to let it interfere with our friendship.

550
00:48:06.987 --> 00:48:13.390
You might be for this one, I might be for that one.

551
00:48:13.390 --> 00:48:20.861
But I still think this is the better man, because if we need something,

552
00:48:20.861 --> 00:48:31.000
we can go to him, somebody know him, and we can get some things did for the community.

553
00:48:31.000 --> 00:48:37.996
He’d be, you know, but he wasn’t a man to blow his own horn about things.

554
00:48:37.996 --> 00:48:45.867
He did what he had to do, he did what he could do, and he just did it.

555
00:48:45.867 --> 00:48:57.236
He ain’t going to say, “I fed all of y’all,” or, “I gave you all some money to go to the football game” and all that.

556
00:48:57.236 --> 00:49:00.297
It was just . . .you’d never hear it.

557
00:49:00.297 --> 00:49:05.106
But a lot of people wouldn’t say that he did it.

558
00:49:05.106 --> 00:49:13.414
I was one of the ones that he did do it for, and I’m not ashamed to say it.

559
00:49:13.414 --> 00:49:15.601
You know what I’m saying?

560
00:49:15.601 --> 00:49:23.034
Because when we first was, when we was building our house over there, I’ll never forget it.

561
00:49:23.034 --> 00:49:28.719
He came to the house and he told my daddy, he said, “Richard,

562
00:49:28.719 --> 00:49:36.589
until you all get your well,” back then, we didn’t have water, you had to dig a well.

563
00:49:36.589 --> 00:49:45.772
He said, “You all can come and get some water, until you all get your well done, dug from the house.”

564
00:49:45.772 --> 00:49:49.270
He said, “You all welcome to do that.”

565
00:49:49.270 --> 00:49:54.517
We didn’t have to ask him, “Could we come get some water?”

566
00:49:54.517 --> 00:49:59.764
Which we could have with our grandmother, but it was much further.

567
00:49:59.764 --> 00:50:08.072
And he’d say, “Until you all dig your well, or whatever you all need some water for around here,

568
00:50:08.072 --> 00:50:17.692
to drink or whatever, clean up with, until you all get your well done, you all could come get water from us.”

569
00:50:17.692 --> 00:50:26.000
We had some five gallon buckets and a wagon, and we’d go get some water from Mr. Valery.

570
00:50:26.000 --> 00:50:32.442
It was like maybe like to that second fence, that’s where we lived at.

571
00:50:32.442 --> 00:50:37.963
I’d say about three houses down from where the old Paradise was.

572
00:50:37.963 --> 00:50:42.104
We’d go get the water from him over there.

573
00:50:42.104 --> 00:50:52.227
And I think it was about, took us about four months to build our, about three or four months to build it.

574
00:50:52.227 --> 00:51:01.890
During that time we’d get some ice and stop there, and my dad and them would fill up the, not the,

575
00:51:01.890 --> 00:51:12.012
wouldn’t [run?] no ice chest, but one old crock pot with some water, and they had some ice, block ice in it.

576
00:51:12.012 --> 00:51:17.074
Because my dad and my uncle and them build the house.

577
00:51:17.074 --> 00:51:22.135
It wasn’t no hire nobody, the contractors come and build it.

578
00:51:22.135 --> 00:51:25.356
They built it in their spare time.

579
00:51:25.356 --> 00:51:31.337
And then we dug the well after they finished, and stuff like that.

580
00:51:31.337 --> 00:51:35.018
My dad did, told him we appreciate everything.

581
00:51:35.018 --> 00:51:41.000
And sometime they would cook, like on a Saturday, Valery or somebody.

582
00:51:41.000 --> 00:51:42.725
<v ARSENEAULT>What would they cook?

583
00:51:42.725 --> 00:51:46.174
<v LEE>Barbecue, like some chicken or make a sauce.

584
00:51:46.174 --> 00:51:49.624
They used to like to make them sauce . . .

585
00:51:49.624 --> 00:51:50.486
<v ARSENEAULT>Sauce piquant?

586
00:51:50.486 --> 00:51:51.349
<v LEE>Sauce piquant.

587
00:51:51.349 --> 00:51:55.661
When they’re working, his wife would make that sauce piquant.

588
00:51:55.661 --> 00:52:02.128
She’d bring everybody a plate, or she’d come by and say, “You all come eat.”

589
00:52:02.128 --> 00:52:04.716
Anybody in Mossville would do that.

590
00:52:04.716 --> 00:52:09.028
“You all got your dinner for the day, or what?

591
00:52:09.028 --> 00:52:12.477
We’re going to bring you all some dinner.”

592
00:52:12.477 --> 00:52:15.927
They kind of talk with their French accent.

593
00:52:15.927 --> 00:52:20.670
And Mama Eula, man, she always had something cooking in there.

594
00:52:20.670 --> 00:52:23.257
We all went to school together.

595
00:52:23.257 --> 00:52:27.569
She had two girls and two boys around our age.

596
00:52:27.569 --> 00:52:38.780
A couple of the boys were older and the girls were younger, by a year or two younger than us, but we all came up together.

597
00:52:38.780 --> 00:52:43.092
And before we got big enough to really go in,

598
00:52:43.092 --> 00:52:52.578
we’d go to Alan and Nelson [Demont?] that afternoon when the dance coming up and you could hear everything outside the building.

599
00:52:52.578 --> 00:52:54.303
You couldn’t go in.

600
00:52:54.303 --> 00:52:58.615
You’re ten, twelve years old, you can’t go in there.

601
00:52:58.615 --> 00:53:04.651
You’re not big enough to, and Mr. Valery say, “No,” or something like that.

602
00:53:04.651 --> 00:53:10.257
“You’re going to get hurt, so you better get out of the way.”

603
00:53:10.257 --> 00:53:15.000
But you could go sit out there on the porch.

604
00:53:15.000 --> 00:53:24.622
They had a porch on the front porch at his house, which they turned into a club later on.

605
00:53:24.622 --> 00:53:26.141
Mr. Caesar did.

606
00:53:26.141 --> 00:53:29.686
You could listen as the musicians played.

607
00:53:29.686 --> 00:53:38.295
And you could go in there when they was practicing during the day and stuff like that.

608
00:53:38.295 --> 00:53:48.423
After you got fourteen, fifteen, sixteen years old, they’d let you go in and work and make a little change.

609
00:53:48.423 --> 00:53:50.955
That was the times, man.

610
00:53:50.955 --> 00:54:04.122
And Etta James and Barbara Lynn, I liked that Barbara Lynn, because she was a lady and she played that guitar and she was left handed.

611
00:54:04.122 --> 00:54:10.705
It just, because she’d just sat there and pick it, man, and sing.

612
00:54:10.705 --> 00:54:14.756
There wasn’t all these electronic stuff back then.

613
00:54:14.756 --> 00:54:17.795
It was doing it from here.

614
00:54:17.795 --> 00:54:20.327
And it was real music.

615
00:54:20.327 --> 00:54:25.897
Joe Tex would bring down the house, him and Joe Simon.

616
00:54:25.897 --> 00:54:28.429
It was a good day.

617
00:54:28.429 --> 00:54:30.962
It was the good days.

618
00:54:30.962 --> 00:54:34.000
It was the good days.

619
00:54:34.000 --> 00:54:35.631
<v ARSENEAULT>Did people dance?

620
00:54:35.631 --> 00:54:36.718
<v LEE>Oh, yeah.

621
00:54:36.718 --> 00:54:39.981
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there names for the dances?

622
00:54:39.981 --> 00:54:45.961
<v LEE>Oh, well they did a two-step, which was the step, basically.

623
00:54:45.961 --> 00:54:59.553
But they always had some kind of new dance, like the watusi and the shuffle and the James Brown thing, and different things like that.

624
00:54:59.553 --> 00:55:01.728
And the funky chicken.

625
00:55:01.728 --> 00:55:03.903
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did people learn?

626
00:55:03.903 --> 00:55:06.078
How did you learn?

627
00:55:06.078 --> 00:55:15.864
<v LEE>Well, you, sometimes you would see it on, somebody was always coming from the city to visit somebody.

628
00:55:15.864 --> 00:55:28.369
And the dances, or they would make a tour, and they had people that was with them, and they would be doing it.

629
00:55:28.369 --> 00:55:30.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Like groupies?

630
00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:30.976
<v LEE>Yeah, groups.

631
00:55:30.976 --> 00:55:42.692
And later on, like this one would have a couple ladies singing, and later on they would break off and start their own group.

632
00:55:42.692 --> 00:55:49.038
Like Al Brass and all of them, they were always somebody’s backup singer.

633
00:55:49.038 --> 00:55:52.943
And then Al took on, on his own.

634
00:55:52.943 --> 00:55:54.408
Just like that.

635
00:55:54.408 --> 00:56:00.754
Then they’d be dancing and they would show different ones and the dances.

636
00:56:00.754 --> 00:56:04.659
And they had teenagers always coming to visit, [ . . . ?].

637
00:56:04.659 --> 00:56:15.886
Because like I said, they had a brain drain, and a lot of the people left and they could come back to visit.

638
00:56:15.886 --> 00:56:22.720
And they would show, we had some canteens back in the day, back then.

639
00:56:22.720 --> 00:56:31.995
Freelows, and Ms. Lula’s and Ms. Martill’s, they had them canteens for the kids would hang out, and dances.

640
00:56:31.995 --> 00:56:42.735
Someone was always showing somebody something, or we would go visit here or visit Houston or somewhere and pick up or something,

641
00:56:42.735 --> 00:56:46.152
they’d come back and they’d show it.

642
00:56:46.152 --> 00:56:52.986
With your proper dancing and all that, we had that at school in P.E.

643
00:56:52.986 --> 00:56:56.403
They had Miss . . . What her name was?

644
00:56:56.403 --> 00:56:57.379
Ms. Dawkins.

645
00:56:57.379 --> 00:57:03.237
Oh, Lord, I can close my eyes, I can see that lady.

646
00:57:03.237 --> 00:57:07.142
But I can’t call her name right now.

647
00:57:07.142 --> 00:57:13.000
But anyway, they were the physical education instructor for the girls.

648
00:57:13.000 --> 00:57:15.892
<v ARSENEAULT>And they learned how to dance?

649
00:57:15.892 --> 00:57:18.301
<v LEE>And they would teach it.

650
00:57:18.301 --> 00:57:26.494
You’d have a dance class and a waltz and stuff like that, and a lot of things.

651
00:57:26.494 --> 00:57:28.422
What’s that woman name?

652
00:57:28.422 --> 00:57:30.831
Why can’t I tell you?

653
00:57:30.831 --> 00:57:33.241
I could remember Ms. Dawkins.

654
00:57:33.241 --> 00:57:34.205
Ms. Madison.

655
00:57:34.205 --> 00:57:35.169
Ms. Madison.

656
00:57:35.169 --> 00:57:39.506
She was from, I think from around Baton Rouge.

657
00:57:39.506 --> 00:57:47.217
And there was always something that they would come back and show the girls, young ladies.

658
00:57:47.217 --> 00:57:56.855
A lot of the time, some of the teachers were fresh out of college, been out a year or two.

659
00:57:56.855 --> 00:58:00.711
They were, I guess we’d say, still clubbing.

660
00:58:00.711 --> 00:58:02.639
Just out of college.

661
00:58:02.639 --> 00:58:07.940
They pretty well knew what was going on in the world.

662
00:58:07.940 --> 00:58:15.169
And they were from all different places, and they would bring their culture to Mossville.

663
00:58:15.169 --> 00:58:19.506
If you want to call it culture or whatever.

664
00:58:19.506 --> 00:58:21.916
You learned a lot from,

665
00:58:21.916 --> 00:58:31.072
man they was . . . And one would show one and it would catch on and everybody would be doing it.

666
00:58:31.072 --> 00:58:33.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you dance?

667
00:58:33.000 --> 00:58:33.576
<v LEE>Yeah.

668
00:58:33.576 --> 00:58:35.305
Not a lot.

669
00:58:35.305 --> 00:58:39.337
But I used to like to dance.

670
00:58:39.337 --> 00:58:47.403
I used to like to two-step, and what they call slow dance, slow drag.

671
00:58:47.403 --> 00:58:50.284
I used to like that.

672
00:58:50.284 --> 00:58:54.893
But that was the thing to do, man,

673
00:58:54.893 --> 00:59:05.840
because they had like teen time at the Catholic school there, Sacred Heart, on Wednesdays, Wednesday night, in Mossville.

674
00:59:05.840 --> 00:59:11.601
And they had those canteens right there by the school.

675
00:59:11.601 --> 00:59:14.481
We used to dance there.

676
00:59:14.481 --> 00:59:18.514
You could go there and have fun.

677
00:59:18.514 --> 00:59:26.004
Ms. Freelow and them had the fried chicken in the canteen, and hamburgers.

678
00:59:26.004 --> 00:59:36.374
Ms. Lula had like the ice cream parlor and hamburgers, but she mostly was an ice cream thing.

679
00:59:36.374 --> 00:59:42.712
Mr. Thomas [Oreese?] had a little canteen across from the school,

680
00:59:42.712 --> 00:59:52.506
and it was mostly dancing and soda pops and cakes and stuff like that, chips and stuff.

681
00:59:52.506 --> 01:00:01.724
Ms. Martill was next door to the barber shop, and mostly hers was open on Sundays,

682
01:00:01.724 --> 01:00:08.062
Saturdays and Sundays, because of the barber shop and the canteen.

683
01:00:08.062 --> 01:00:15.551
It was basically a store during the week, Monday through Friday and Sunday.

684
01:00:15.551 --> 01:00:20.737
Sunday they really, it was just for the canteen.

685
01:00:20.737 --> 01:00:34.564
But Saturday evening, it was a little dance and she had right there at the barber shop, in the back of the barber shop.

686
01:00:34.564 --> 01:00:38.597
Mr. Benson, Lorenzo Benson, was the barber.

687
01:00:38.597 --> 01:00:44.358
Later on, Mr. Thibodaux opened a barber shop down there.

688
01:00:44.358 --> 01:00:50.119
And [Belle Aqueese barber?] but Uncle [Brock?] had them retire.

689
01:00:50.119 --> 01:00:53.000
But that was a . . .

690
01:00:53.000 --> 01:00:56.176
<v ARSENEAULT>Who went to the barber shop?

691
01:00:56.176 --> 01:00:56.706
<v LEE>[Pardon?]

692
01:00:56.706 --> 01:00:59.882
<v ARSENEAULT>Who went to the barber shop?

693
01:00:59.882 --> 01:01:03.588
<v LEE>Well, the men, the boys, young men.

694
01:01:03.588 --> 01:01:08.353
Because they had that Afro thing going back then.

695
01:01:08.353 --> 01:01:16.294
You keep your hair fluffed out, and keep it lined up, keep it up straight.

696
01:01:16.294 --> 01:01:23.706
The Afro was a big deal back in the ‘60s, and the early ‘70s.

697
01:01:23.706 --> 01:01:25.294
Get it trimmed.

698
01:01:25.294 --> 01:01:30.588
Keep your hair . . . And then the Ivy League, the close-cut.

699
01:01:30.588 --> 01:01:34.824
You had to have a fresh haircut, man.

700
01:01:34.824 --> 01:01:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have an Afro?

701
01:01:38.000 --> 01:01:41.105
<v LEE>Yeah, at one time, I did.

702
01:01:41.105 --> 01:01:41.623
Yeah.

703
01:01:41.623 --> 01:01:44.728
I liked the Ivy League cut.

704
01:01:44.728 --> 01:01:51.456
And then the Afro took off in the late ‘60s, about the mid-‘60s.

705
01:01:51.456 --> 01:01:57.667
That’s when most of them start wearing the Afro, in the ‘60s.

706
01:01:57.667 --> 01:02:04.395
But I started shaving my head after I came out of the military.

707
01:02:04.395 --> 01:02:13.193
A couple years after I came out of the military, I started just shaving it all off.

708
01:02:13.193 --> 01:02:18.886
It’s not that it won’t grow or I’m bald-headed or nothing.

709
01:02:18.886 --> 01:02:20.956
I just prefer that.

710
01:02:20.956 --> 01:02:31.825
My grandfather was a bald-headed, bald head, and I just said it was a Lee thing, and I bald my head.

711
01:02:31.825 --> 01:02:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So where did the women get their hair done?

712
01:02:37.000 --> 01:02:40.304
<v LEE>Ms. Rigmaiden had a beauty shop.

713
01:02:40.304 --> 01:02:43.057
Ms. Joanne Rigmaiden had one.

714
01:02:43.057 --> 01:02:55.721
And later on, Mrs. Linda Louis had a beauty shop there at the end of Michigan [Avenue], right off the Old Spanish Trail.

715
01:02:55.721 --> 01:03:00.676
She had about a three or four-chair beauty shop.

716
01:03:00.676 --> 01:03:08.385
And my aunt Artemis Irving, she had a beauty shop built onto her house.

717
01:03:08.385 --> 01:03:14.992
She had a shop there in Mossville off the Old Spanish Trail.

718
01:03:14.992 --> 01:03:23.802
Most of the people on that end of town in the Mossville went to Ms. Irving,

719
01:03:23.802 --> 01:03:33.713
Aunt Ida, we called her Aunt Ida because of my grandmother’s sister, my great-aunt, she was an Irving.

720
01:03:33.713 --> 01:03:35.915
She married Bernard Irving.

721
01:03:35.915 --> 01:03:43.073
But anyway, she did a lot of the styling back in the day.

722
01:03:43.073 --> 01:03:56.287
When the women were cutting their hair with the bobs and all of that, she was one of the ones that would do it.

723
01:03:56.287 --> 01:04:00.142
And then she, all of them retired.

724
01:04:00.142 --> 01:04:08.951
And Linda had, Linda Louis had the big shop down across from Bel Air down there,

725
01:04:08.951 --> 01:04:16.660
right off of Michigan, at the end of Michigan and the Old Spanish Trail.

726
01:04:16.660 --> 01:04:21.615
She had a big, three or four-chair beauty shop.

727
01:04:21.615 --> 01:04:25.470
She had it for a long time.

728
01:04:25.470 --> 01:04:38.134
And I think Conoco, during the time I sold out, probably bought her out then because she moved and she went to Lafayette.

729
01:04:38.134 --> 01:04:47.494
That was in 2000 or something like that, because ‘99 was when I did this up here.

730
01:04:47.494 --> 01:04:53.000
I sold out to Conoco over there in ‘99.

731
01:04:53.000 --> 01:05:01.087
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there ever like a unisex, or like both gender barber shop or anything?

732
01:05:01.087 --> 01:05:12.063
<v LEE>Well, they would cut men hair, too, especially with, but specialized with the women, there were the young ladies.

733
01:05:12.063 --> 01:05:20.728
They would . . . And her husband ran that little sandwich shop or food shop next door.

734
01:05:20.728 --> 01:05:21.306
Lawrence.

735
01:05:21.306 --> 01:05:32.859
He worked at the plant, but he had a little barbecue stand next door on like Thursday, Friday and Saturdays.

736
01:05:32.859 --> 01:05:40.369
He would make barbecue sandwiches and stuff like that, and we’d hang out.

737
01:05:40.369 --> 01:05:50.189
It wasn’t a club, but it was go there and have coffee and sat around and talked.

738
01:05:50.189 --> 01:05:54.811
It was a gathering place for the men.

739
01:05:54.811 --> 01:06:06.364
In fact, I owned a little pub there for several years there in the Mossville area on the First Avenue.

740
01:06:06.364 --> 01:06:09.252
Had a little neighborhood bar.

741
01:06:09.252 --> 01:06:13.296
We’d open like from Wednesday to Saturday.

742
01:06:13.296 --> 01:06:15.607
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it called?

743
01:06:15.607 --> 01:06:16.762
<v LEE>The Pub.

744
01:06:16.762 --> 01:06:17.917
<v ARSENEAULT>The Pub?

745
01:06:17.917 --> 01:06:18.495
<v LEE>Yeah.

746
01:06:18.495 --> 01:06:25.427
It was where the old country club used to be in Mossville.

747
01:06:25.427 --> 01:06:27.160
I owned it.

748
01:06:27.160 --> 01:06:34.670
One of my friends, Donny Mouton, one of my, we grew up together.

749
01:06:34.670 --> 01:06:37.558
He was kind of disabled.

750
01:06:37.558 --> 01:06:42.757
He ran it for me, but I owned it.

751
01:06:42.757 --> 01:06:52.000
And we had it there for several years there on First Avenue and Bel Air.

752
01:06:52.000 --> 01:06:55.758
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things did you serve?

753
01:06:55.758 --> 01:06:58.978
<v LEE>We served mostly beer and whiskey.

754
01:06:58.978 --> 01:07:07.030
Every Wednesday we would cook, or Thursday we would cook a sauce, or cook something.

755
01:07:07.030 --> 01:07:12.398
Had pretty good business and good clientele, mostly working class.

756
01:07:12.398 --> 01:07:18.840
They would hang around, they’d come on Thursdays and we’d cook supper.

757
01:07:18.840 --> 01:07:21.524
We started it on Wednesday.

758
01:07:21.524 --> 01:07:29.039
We had a pool table in there and we had pool tournaments and beer.

759
01:07:29.039 --> 01:07:29.576
It.

760
01:07:29.576 --> 01:07:37.091
I’d say a couple of years, for about three or four, about four years,

761
01:07:37.091 --> 01:07:44.069
for sure, and then I shut it down and they remodeled the building.

762
01:07:44.069 --> 01:07:48.900
But it was some dispute about them, the building.

763
01:07:48.900 --> 01:07:51.048
I was leasing it.

764
01:07:51.048 --> 01:07:59.100
And I got ready to try to reopen it, and they had built the building.

765
01:07:59.100 --> 01:08:02.857
Something about the [boundaries?] in the building.

766
01:08:02.857 --> 01:08:13.593
And they never, nobody never could open it up because they never did get it straight about the property lines.

767
01:08:13.593 --> 01:08:16.814
So I didn’t open back up.

768
01:08:16.814 --> 01:08:17.887
Nobody did.

769
01:08:17.887 --> 01:08:21.645
And I was renting it, leasing it.

770
01:08:21.645 --> 01:08:25.403
I was renting it or leasing it.

771
01:08:25.403 --> 01:08:29.697
No one never did open it back up.

772
01:08:29.697 --> 01:08:37.212
I couldn’t open it up because they were in dispute about the property line.

773
01:08:37.212 --> 01:08:40.970
They never did go to the courts.

774
01:08:40.970 --> 01:08:49.558
I don’t . . . I really didn’t follow up on it because I was so disgusted about it.

775
01:08:49.558 --> 01:08:52.242
I just let it go.

776
01:08:52.242 --> 01:08:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So what happened to the land?

777
01:08:56.000 --> 01:09:03.777
<v LEE>I guess . . . I don't know if they ever got it straight, but I’m pretty sure Sasol did.

778
01:09:03.777 --> 01:09:07.437
It was the dispute about the property line.

779
01:09:07.437 --> 01:09:12.927
They said that the building that he rebuilt exceeded this property line.

780
01:09:12.927 --> 01:09:21.162
And he had some kind of injunction to stop it from opening, so we couldn’t open back up.

781
01:09:21.162 --> 01:09:22.992
I checked into it.

782
01:09:22.992 --> 01:09:28.939
I waited and waited and waited, but they never did get it straight.

783
01:09:28.939 --> 01:09:35.344
So I just said, well, forget it, because I was already working two jobs.

784
01:09:35.344 --> 01:09:42.206
And my buddy that was running it for me, we just didn’t pursue it anymore.

785
01:09:42.206 --> 01:09:49.526
I tried to buy it out from both of them, buy his land and his building.

786
01:09:49.526 --> 01:09:56.846
And one say, “It’s on my property, that’s my building, part of the building is mine.”

787
01:09:56.846 --> 01:10:06.453
And I’d say, “Well, if you all could come to some kind of agreement about some kind of price, reasonable price,

788
01:10:06.453 --> 01:10:16.518
I would buy it, buy your land and your part of the building, and your land and your part of the building.”

789
01:10:16.518 --> 01:10:19.721
But I never could get it straight.

790
01:10:19.721 --> 01:10:27.498
In fact, a lawyer, I contacted him about the situation, but they never could get it straight.

791
01:10:27.498 --> 01:10:31.615
One didn’t want to give in to the other.

792
01:10:31.615 --> 01:10:40.765
So I just told my buddy, “Don’t worry about it, because,” I say, “I’m running enough as it is now.”

793
01:10:40.765 --> 01:10:45.340
<v ARSENEAULT>Well we are almost to an hour and a half.

794
01:10:45.340 --> 01:10:49.000
<v LEE>Any other questions you want to ask?

795
01:10:49.000 --> 01:10:51.564
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah, I have some.

796
01:10:51.564 --> 01:10:57.333
There’s one I wanted to ask about, let’s see.

797
01:10:57.333 --> 01:11:05.667
When did you all find out that the water table was being polluted?

798
01:11:05.667 --> 01:11:08.231
<v LEE>Exactly, I don't know.

799
01:11:08.231 --> 01:11:12.718
But it was in the early ‘70s.

800
01:11:12.718 --> 01:11:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

801
01:11:14.000 --> 01:11:18.464
<v LEE>It was in the ‘70s, early ‘70s.

802
01:11:18.464 --> 01:11:33.768
Because when I came back, if I’m not mistaken, in 1970 I came back from Vietnam, there was a big discussion about the water.

803
01:11:33.768 --> 01:11:44.609
They had the board together and everything, and they were in operations or, I can’t remember exactly.

804
01:11:44.609 --> 01:11:49.710
But I know it’s in the early ‘70s.

805
01:11:49.710 --> 01:11:52.261
You got another one?

806
01:11:52.261 --> 01:11:52.899
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

807
01:11:52.899 --> 01:11:53.536
Sorry.

808
01:11:53.536 --> 01:11:58.000
So how did they find out?

809
01:11:58.000 --> 01:12:02.613
<v LEE>I don't know exactly how they found out about it.

810
01:12:02.613 --> 01:12:10.916
They seeing, they started seeing a lot of the people were having cancer and they came to thinking.

811
01:12:10.916 --> 01:12:18.759
They had some independent people done some, come out and did some tests on the water system.

812
01:12:18.759 --> 01:12:29.368
The government, a lot of them were there before the water system went in that was using those shallow wells, to my understanding.

813
01:12:29.368 --> 01:12:41.362
If you’re going to drink out of this shallow well for twenty years, we’ve had this water system for four or five years, well, I mean,

814
01:12:41.362 --> 01:12:50.127
how can you tell it came from this well or it came from your other ten years or whatever,

815
01:12:50.127 --> 01:12:53.817
five years that you drunk this other water?

816
01:12:53.817 --> 01:12:56.124
You understand what I’m saying?

817
01:12:56.124 --> 01:13:01.659
It’s kind of which one came first, the cart or the horse.

818
01:13:01.659 --> 01:13:03.966
You know what I’m saying?

819
01:13:03.966 --> 01:13:07.656
Because to my understanding, I might be wrong,

820
01:13:07.656 --> 01:13:16.421
but if you going to eat this over here for five years, and then this other once you stop,

821
01:13:16.421 --> 01:13:28.415
and then you start eating this over here, now for a year or two, and you’re going to say this is the one making you sick.

822
01:13:28.415 --> 01:13:30.721
You’ve eaten both of them.

823
01:13:30.721 --> 01:13:35.334
So I mean, which tree did the pollutant come from?

824
01:13:35.334 --> 01:13:42.254
From the shallow well living, from the environment, from the air, or from the water?

825
01:13:42.254 --> 01:13:49.635
The water might be bad, but the government is testing it and . . . I couldn’t see it.

826
01:13:49.635 --> 01:13:53.786
You know what I’m saying, what I’m talking about?

827
01:13:53.786 --> 01:13:59.783
If the water that bad, why the government keep letting us drink it?

828
01:13:59.783 --> 01:14:02.090
You know what I’m saying?

829
01:14:02.090 --> 01:14:08.087
I mean, it ain’t like they didn’t come out and do some testing.

830
01:14:08.087 --> 01:14:11.316
So it was an information gap there.

831
01:14:11.316 --> 01:14:15.929
I lived there and I always did drink the water.

832
01:14:15.929 --> 01:14:27.000
Because I say, hell, I’ve been here, I’m twenty-some years old, I’ve been drinking this water all my life, I ain’t dead yet.

833
01:14:27.000 --> 01:14:32.063
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you all treat it or anything when you all drank it?

834
01:14:32.063 --> 01:14:37.970
<v LEE>Not back then, no. But after the water system started, they started treating it.

835
01:14:37.970 --> 01:14:43.034
We had a damn thing you pumped like that, the old-time pump.

836
01:14:43.034 --> 01:14:45.143
I’m sixty-seven, sixty-eight years old.

837
01:14:45.143 --> 01:14:49.363
Back then, when I was born, they had a pump.

838
01:14:49.363 --> 01:14:57.380
And then my grandfather had a well where you dropped the bucket in there and it would fill up,

839
01:14:57.380 --> 01:15:05.819
and then you’d pull it up, and you’d dump it out, the thing in your bucket, and carry it out.

840
01:15:05.819 --> 01:15:13.835
They had a big old crock pot, the crock, that they would tie a straining rag, I called it,

841
01:15:13.835 --> 01:15:22.696
and they would pour it in there, and it would strain it, the water, as it poured it in the crock.

842
01:15:22.696 --> 01:15:30.291
You had dipper, you’d dip it in, you’d get you some water and pour it in a glass.

843
01:15:30.291 --> 01:15:33.245
But plants kept growing and getting bigger.

844
01:15:33.245 --> 01:15:35.776
And the water system came in.

845
01:15:35.776 --> 01:15:38.308
And they said it’s the water.

846
01:15:38.308 --> 01:15:47.591
I mean, you’ve lived in California twenty years, then you’re going to come back here and tell us this is what’s happening?

847
01:15:47.591 --> 01:15:49.700
And we making you sick?

848
01:15:49.700 --> 01:15:57.295
How do you know it wasn’t something over there you got a hold of that’s making you sick?

849
01:15:57.295 --> 01:16:07.000
Because everything that you take in like that, it takes, like asbestos, it takes ten, twenty years sometimes before it show up.

850
01:16:07.000 --> 01:16:09.628
I’m not going to dispute you.

851
01:16:09.628 --> 01:16:14.885
But I’m not going to jump on the bandwagon with you, either.

852
01:16:14.885 --> 01:16:17.076
I mean, I ain’t crazy.

853
01:16:17.076 --> 01:16:19.266
You know what I’m saying?

854
01:16:19.266 --> 01:16:21.018
I might be wrong.

855
01:16:21.018 --> 01:16:22.770
You might be right.

856
01:16:22.770 --> 01:16:30.218
But I can’t see the government letting us drink this water if it’s not, if it’s contaminated.

857
01:16:30.218 --> 01:16:31.970
I can’t see it.

858
01:16:31.970 --> 01:16:41.607
I mean, I don't think that the government is that bad, because I’ve seen bad shit across the water, excuse my French.

859
01:16:41.607 --> 01:16:52.997
It changed my ideas thinking about a lot of things, to see the way people lived over there compared to the way we lived over here,

860
01:16:52.997 --> 01:16:58.692
even though we was treated bad, you know, the blacks were treated bad.

861
01:16:58.692 --> 01:17:00.006
It wasn’t fair.

862
01:17:00.006 --> 01:17:02.196
But look around over there.

863
01:17:02.196 --> 01:17:06.577
And I said, “Man, thank God for the United States.”

864
01:17:06.577 --> 01:17:16.215
Even though I might have been, couldn’t go in that door over there, and had to take the door on the left,

865
01:17:16.215 --> 01:17:22.785
but compared to what’s going on over here, give me the door on the left.

866
01:17:22.785 --> 01:17:28.042
And it opened my eyes about a lot, a lot of things.

867
01:17:28.042 --> 01:17:34.613
But it also made me understand that I’m not like, my grandfather used to say,

868
01:17:34.613 --> 01:17:40.308
“I’m not going to bend my neck so you could ride my back.”

869
01:17:40.308 --> 01:17:41.622
Do you understand?

870
01:17:41.622 --> 01:17:45.127
Now I fought and killed for this country.

871
01:17:45.127 --> 01:17:46.879
I want what’s mine.

872
01:17:46.879 --> 01:17:56.955
I’m not going to the door on the left just because you say I’ve got to go to the door on the left.

873
01:17:56.955 --> 01:17:59.145
I’m going in the door.

874
01:17:59.145 --> 01:18:06.154
You try to beat me up or send me to jail, whatever you want to do.

875
01:18:06.154 --> 01:18:09.221
But I killed people for this country.

876
01:18:09.221 --> 01:18:10.973
This is my country.

877
01:18:10.973 --> 01:18:11.849
You understand?

878
01:18:11.849 --> 01:18:16.668
I laid down my life and took life for this country,

879
01:18:16.668 --> 01:18:26.305
and I’m not going to let you tell me or dictate to me what I can do and what I can’t do.

880
01:18:26.305 --> 01:18:27.181
You understand?

881
01:18:27.181 --> 01:18:32.000
And I mean that from the bottom of my heart.

882
01:18:32.000 --> 01:18:39.556
Just like I was working for the sheriff’s office [ . . . ?]. [Dusay?] told me, “You’re not bringing me to jail.”

883
01:18:39.556 --> 01:18:40.816
I said, “What?”

884
01:18:40.816 --> 01:18:45.433
He said, so and so, “You ain’t taking me to jail.”

885
01:18:45.433 --> 01:18:49.212
And I said, “Well, I’ll tell you one thing.

886
01:18:49.212 --> 01:18:53.410
I can show you better than I can tell you.”

887
01:18:53.410 --> 01:18:58.867
I said, “Ninety-two sheriff’s office, send me an ambulance, 1079, to this location.”

888
01:18:58.867 --> 01:19:00.966
He said, “What you calling?

889
01:19:00.966 --> 01:19:02.645
You better be calling [?].”

890
01:19:02.645 --> 01:19:06.003
I said, “No, I’m calling for an ambulance.”

891
01:19:06.003 --> 01:19:10.201
He said, “What are you calling for an ambulance for?”

892
01:19:10.201 --> 01:19:14.399
I said, “One of us is going to need it.

893
01:19:14.399 --> 01:19:17.758
More than likely, it’s going to be you.”

894
01:19:17.758 --> 01:19:19.437
He said, “Let’s go.”

895
01:19:19.437 --> 01:19:23.215
I say, “Man, you messing with the wrong one.”

896
01:19:23.215 --> 01:19:26.573
I say, “I’m here to do a job.

897
01:19:26.573 --> 01:19:29.512
And I’m going to do my job.

898
01:19:29.512 --> 01:19:32.451
If you run, you’re going to jail.”

899
01:19:32.451 --> 01:19:37.488
But he wasn’t used to nobody my color standing up to him.

900
01:19:37.488 --> 01:19:40.427
I said, “I’m not afraid of you.

901
01:19:40.427 --> 01:19:44.625
And I don’t want you to be afraid of me.

902
01:19:44.625 --> 01:19:53.020
But I’m here to do what I’m supposed to do, and I think I’m supposed to take you to jail.

903
01:19:53.020 --> 01:19:56.379
And I’m going to take you to jail.

904
01:19:56.379 --> 01:20:01.416
Or I’m going to take you to the hospital, then to jail.

905
01:20:01.416 --> 01:20:07.294
Or that ambulance going to be here to take care of me or you.

906
01:20:07.294 --> 01:20:15.689
But one of us going to need it if you don’t want to do what I’m asking you to do.”

907
01:20:15.689 --> 01:20:19.048
So after that, I had very little trouble.

908
01:20:19.048 --> 01:20:21.567
I told him I meant that.

909
01:20:21.567 --> 01:20:24.085
Because I got counseled about it.

910
01:20:24.085 --> 01:20:27.024
They called me in, talked to me.

911
01:20:27.024 --> 01:20:31.222
I told them, “Yes, this is what I told him.”

912
01:20:31.222 --> 01:20:35.000
I said, “I’d tell you the same thing.”

913
01:20:35.000 --> 01:20:37.096
<v ARSENEAULT>What was your circuit?

914
01:20:37.096 --> 01:20:37.620
<v LEE>[Pardon?]

915
01:20:37.620 --> 01:20:40.764
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have like a circuit?

916
01:20:40.764 --> 01:20:43.907
Or what do they call it?

917
01:20:43.907 --> 01:20:47.575
<v LEE>I was a deputy throughout Calcasieu Parish.

918
01:20:47.575 --> 01:20:53.863
But I became a resident deputy in Mossville for sixteen, seventeen years.

919
01:20:53.863 --> 01:20:58.054
But when I first started, I was parish-wide.

920
01:20:58.054 --> 01:20:59.102
You understand?

921
01:20:59.102 --> 01:21:03.818
So they’d send you anywhere, different places and everything.

922
01:21:03.818 --> 01:21:10.629
And you know how it was back in the ‘60s and the ‘70s.

923
01:21:10.629 --> 01:21:19.013
Well in the ‘70s, early ‘70s, about the mid-‘70s, I start working for the sheriff’s office.

924
01:21:19.013 --> 01:21:22.681
And it wasn’t all over the parish.

925
01:21:22.681 --> 01:21:28.968
Some places they didn’t respect the black man to be a man.

926
01:21:28.968 --> 01:21:34.732
You had the incidents throughout life where they’d challenge you sometimes.

927
01:21:34.732 --> 01:21:37.351
You know what I’m saying?

928
01:21:37.351 --> 01:21:39.971
Or say stuff to you.

929
01:21:39.971 --> 01:21:43.115
But they couldn’t back it up.

930
01:21:43.115 --> 01:21:45.735
It’s like I told them.

931
01:21:45.735 --> 01:21:49.927
It’s just like when I moved up here.

932
01:21:49.927 --> 01:21:53.070
I’ll give you a for instance.

933
01:21:53.070 --> 01:22:00.406
Me and one of my friends, we were building some stalls in my barn.

934
01:22:00.406 --> 01:22:05.121
And they had a truck kept passing, kept passing.

935
01:22:05.121 --> 01:22:09.313
So we left to go get some dinner.

936
01:22:09.313 --> 01:22:15.601
So we coming up, walking up the driveway, the van pulled in.

937
01:22:15.601 --> 01:22:19.268
And the man say, “Hey, you boys!”

938
01:22:19.268 --> 01:22:23.460
I’m six foot-five, three hundred and some pounds.

939
01:22:23.460 --> 01:22:27.652
I looked at my partner, I looked around.

940
01:22:27.652 --> 01:22:31.319
He said, “No, I’m talking to y’all.

941
01:22:31.319 --> 01:22:32.367
To you.”

942
01:22:32.367 --> 01:22:33.939
I said, “Yeah?”

943
01:22:33.939 --> 01:22:37.083
He had his wife with him.

944
01:22:37.083 --> 01:22:45.466
Before I can say anything, he said, “I’m looking for that man you boys working for.”

945
01:22:45.466 --> 01:22:47.038
I say, “Yeah?”

946
01:22:47.038 --> 01:22:56.994
He say, “I want to invite him to church Sunday.” [laughs] I look at my buddy and I laugh.

947
01:22:56.994 --> 01:23:02.233
I say, “Mister,” . . . I can’t say what I told him.

948
01:23:02.233 --> 01:23:07.473
But anyway, I say, “I’m the M.F. that owns this.”

949
01:23:07.473 --> 01:23:19.000
And I said . . . He said, “Well, you don’t have to be cursing and don’t say that in front of my wife.”

950
01:23:19.000 --> 01:23:21.943
I said, “Well, let me tell you something.

951
01:23:21.943 --> 01:23:23.415
You’re on my property.

952
01:23:23.415 --> 01:23:26.358
And this is America, last time I checked.”

953
01:23:26.358 --> 01:23:30.038
I say, “I fought and killed people for this country.”

954
01:23:30.038 --> 01:23:37.396
And I say, “And I’m going to let you travel up here to my house and call me a boy?

955
01:23:37.396 --> 01:23:43.651
I’m six-foot five, three hundred and some pounds, 325 pounds, and I’m not a man to you?”

956
01:23:43.651 --> 01:23:50.642
I say, “Well, you get out of that van and you show me how much a man you is.”

957
01:23:50.642 --> 01:23:54.689
“I still don’t like you cursing in front of my wife!”

958
01:23:54.689 --> 01:23:56.528
I say, “You cursed me.

959
01:23:56.528 --> 01:24:02.783
You called me a boy.” [mimics more yelling] I say, “Look, let me tell you something, mister.

960
01:24:02.783 --> 01:24:05.726
Best you do, take you and your wife.”

961
01:24:05.726 --> 01:24:14.557
I said, “And if you come here again, you leave her home if you don’t want to hear what I got to tell you.

962
01:24:14.557 --> 01:24:17.500
Because what I told you is not Christian-like.

963
01:24:17.500 --> 01:24:18.972
I know it’s not.

964
01:24:18.972 --> 01:24:26.330
But it wasn’t Christian-like for you to come here and you being a preacher, calling a black man a boy.

965
01:24:26.330 --> 01:24:27.434
Two of them.

966
01:24:27.434 --> 01:24:28.170
You understand?

967
01:24:28.170 --> 01:24:37.000
And asking me where the white man at that own this, like a black man can’t have nothing like a place like this.”

968
01:24:37.000 --> 01:24:46.297
I say, I told him, my friend, I say, “That was one of the worst things,

969
01:24:46.297 --> 01:24:56.176
to me, when I came back from the war and the way people tried to treat me.

970
01:24:56.176 --> 01:25:03.149
That I know what I went through and sacrificed for this country.

971
01:25:03.149 --> 01:25:17.095
And then somebody come back and do something like that to you, that hadn’t even been in the Girl Scouts or the Boy Scouts.”

972
01:25:17.095 --> 01:25:26.973
I say, “That was the most traumatic thing for me to handle and control myself was that.”

973
01:25:26.973 --> 01:25:30.459
From time to time, it happens.

974
01:25:30.459 --> 01:25:36.270
But it got to where I could deal with it.

975
01:25:36.270 --> 01:25:38.595
But that was hard.

976
01:25:38.595 --> 01:25:40.338
That was hard.

977
01:25:40.338 --> 01:25:41.500
Really hard.

978
01:25:41.500 --> 01:25:47.892
You come from a generation, you don’t know nothing about that.

979
01:25:47.892 --> 01:25:58.351
When I was in college, they had places you couldn’t stop to get, to eat and buy gas.

980
01:25:58.351 --> 01:26:03.000
They had a sign, “No Ns allowed.

981
01:26:03.000 --> 01:26:09.865
<v ARSENEAULT>I remember reading about Coach Robinson would make the boys sandwiches because he wasn’t allowed to stop.

982
01:26:09.865 --> 01:26:11.481
<v LEE>Wasn’t allowed to stop.

983
01:26:11.481 --> 01:26:18.750
But you had to, you could take me over there and make me kill people for this country,

984
01:26:18.750 --> 01:26:24.404
and then you’re going to want me to come back and bend my neck?

985
01:26:24.404 --> 01:26:27.231
No. No. I’m not bending my neck.

986
01:26:27.231 --> 01:26:30.865
I’ll be carried by eight or juried by twelve.

987
01:26:30.865 --> 01:26:34.904
I’m not going to, I’m not going to take it.

988
01:26:34.904 --> 01:26:39.346
And I’m not going to, let you know how I feel.

989
01:26:39.346 --> 01:26:42.577
I’m going to tell you how I feel.

990
01:26:42.577 --> 01:26:42.981
Yeah.

991
01:26:42.981 --> 01:26:45.000
It was bad, baby.

992
01:26:45.000 --> 01:26:49.085
But I could see the light.

993
01:26:49.085 --> 01:27:02.019
To live in Mossville, and to see . . . God let me live to see a black man as the president.

994
01:27:02.019 --> 01:27:06.104
I thought this would never happen.

995
01:27:06.104 --> 01:27:12.231
I seen Martin Luther King in person in Arkansas.

996
01:27:12.231 --> 01:27:26.527
I could see where we were trying to go, and I seen all the obstacles in the road to get there.

997
01:27:26.527 --> 01:27:40.142
I get emotional about that, because it was such a tragic and dirty road to travel to get this far,

998
01:27:40.142 --> 01:27:46.269
and to see that everything that has happened, happened.

999
01:27:46.269 --> 01:27:56.481
And to see him make it to that office, is just . . . hell, I don't know.

1000
01:27:56.481 --> 01:28:01.246
I say, this really is a miracle.

1001
01:28:01.246 --> 01:28:06.692
Because I thought I would never see it.

1002
01:28:06.692 --> 01:28:09.415
Not in my lifetime.

1003
01:28:09.415 --> 01:28:13.500
I wasn’t sure it wouldn’t happen.

1004
01:28:13.500 --> 01:28:28.477
But when they first told me that Obama was running for president, I just, I just, I couldn’t see him making it.

1005
01:28:28.477 --> 01:28:34.604
I could see him being able to be president.

1006
01:28:34.604 --> 01:28:45.496
But the attitude of the people from generations past, that somebody would try to assassinate him.

1007
01:28:45.496 --> 01:28:50.262
And I thought he would get assassinated.

1008
01:28:50.262 --> 01:29:00.473
Because of the way that Kennedy, you understand, and how they did Martin Luther King.

1009
01:29:00.473 --> 01:29:07.281
And then it showed me that hey, it can happen.

1010
01:29:07.281 --> 01:29:10.004
That he did win.

1011
01:29:10.004 --> 01:29:16.131
He stayed in there, won it, not one time.

1012
01:29:16.131 --> 01:29:21.577
It was no accident; he did it twice.

1013
01:29:21.577 --> 01:29:29.746
And I said there’s really got to be a God up there.

1014
01:29:29.746 --> 01:29:33.831
Because I just . . . I don't know.

1015
01:29:33.831 --> 01:29:36.554
People change, times change.

1016
01:29:36.554 --> 01:29:42.000
I just never thought I’d see it.

1017
01:29:42.000 --> 01:29:46.250
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about Dr. King?

1018
01:29:46.250 --> 01:29:48.679
<v LEE>There’s a story there.

1019
01:29:48.679 --> 01:29:52.321
But he was a remarkable man.

1020
01:29:52.321 --> 01:29:58.393
Because I’m not the one to turn the other cheek.

1021
01:29:58.393 --> 01:30:06.893
I might turn it, but soon as I turn it, I’m turning it on.

1022
01:30:06.893 --> 01:30:16.607
Because some people only, that’s the only thing you can get their attention with is firepower.

1023
01:30:16.607 --> 01:30:20.250
But he got it with kindness.

1024
01:30:20.250 --> 01:30:26.321
And I got a certain amount of kindness to me,

1025
01:30:26.321 --> 01:30:36.643
but I kind of believe in an eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth.

1026
01:30:36.643 --> 01:30:42.714
If you hit me, I’m going to hit you back.

1027
01:30:42.714 --> 01:30:56.679
I might turn the other cheek, but soon as I turn the other cheek and you hit me, I’m going to hit you.

1028
01:30:56.679 --> 01:31:08.821
But he was a very remarkable man, because he took . . . Man, they spit on him, they did everything to him.

1029
01:31:08.821 --> 01:31:12.464
And they ended up killing him.

1030
01:31:12.464 --> 01:31:19.143
I thought that was what was going to happen to Obama.

1031
01:31:19.143 --> 01:31:27.036
In fact, he really should have been one of the first black presidents.

1032
01:31:27.036 --> 01:31:41.607
He had that intelligence about him, because he could, he held millions back in the violence that was about to take place in America.

1033
01:31:41.607 --> 01:31:50.107
He controlled a whole lot of black soldiers that he made them stand down.

1034
01:31:50.107 --> 01:31:57.393
Because it was going to be some real trouble here in America.

1035
01:31:57.393 --> 01:31:59.214
Which there was.

1036
01:31:59.214 --> 01:32:05.286
But he made them stand down, and that was remarkable.

1037
01:32:05.286 --> 01:32:12.571
He said that we was going to win this thing in kindness.

1038
01:32:12.571 --> 01:32:22.286
See, it might take a little longer, but it’s not going to take as much blood.

1039
01:32:22.286 --> 01:32:24.107
And it did.

1040
01:32:24.107 --> 01:32:27.143
But then, and I just . . .

1041
01:32:27.143 --> 01:32:30.786
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever hear him speak?

1042
01:32:30.786 --> 01:32:32.000
<v LEE>[Pardon?]

1043
01:32:32.000 --> 01:32:34.778
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you hear him speak?

1044
01:32:34.778 --> 01:32:35.333
<v LEE>Yeah.

1045
01:32:35.333 --> 01:32:38.111
I saw him in Arkansas.

1046
01:32:38.111 --> 01:32:39.222
<v ARSENEAULT>Where in . . .

1047
01:32:39.222 --> 01:32:40.889
<v LEE>In the ‘60s.

1048
01:32:40.889 --> 01:32:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1049
01:32:42.000 --> 01:32:48.375
<v LEE>And he’s talking about getting into all walks of life.

1050
01:32:48.375 --> 01:32:52.199
Don’t just be preachers and teachers.

1051
01:32:52.199 --> 01:32:59.211
We need firemen, policemen, we need this and we need that.

1052
01:32:59.211 --> 01:33:03.036
And operators, draftsmen, doctors and all.

1053
01:33:03.036 --> 01:33:06.861
Get into all walks of life.

1054
01:33:06.861 --> 01:33:17.060
During those times, to make a long story short, I got out, I didn’t do anything,

1055
01:33:17.060 --> 01:33:28.535
but I was accused of doing something that I didn’t do, and the police kind of mishandled me.

1056
01:33:28.535 --> 01:33:31.722
<v ARSENEAULT>This was in the ‘60s?

1057
01:33:31.722 --> 01:33:32.360
<v LEE>Yeah.

1058
01:33:32.360 --> 01:33:38.097
So I mean, I got drafted in the military.

1059
01:33:38.097 --> 01:33:47.659
Everything, my grandfather say, “Things that happen to you, happen to you for a reason.”

1060
01:33:47.659 --> 01:34:01.683
So after I got in there and everything, I was in training, they offered me to go through the federal investigators’ school.

1061
01:34:01.683 --> 01:34:05.508
He said, “You would work undercover.”

1062
01:34:05.508 --> 01:34:10.607
“Well I don’t want to be no police.”

1063
01:34:10.607 --> 01:34:16.982
He said, “None of this would be on your record.

1064
01:34:16.982 --> 01:34:28.456
You’re just undercover, you write [letters?], they give you an address that you would write to and everything.

1065
01:34:28.456 --> 01:34:37.381
You’d just be like a . . .” I said, “You want me to be a snitch!

1066
01:34:37.381 --> 01:34:41.205
I won’t be no damn snitch!”

1067
01:34:41.205 --> 01:34:50.130
That night, man, I went to bed and I tried to go to sleep.

1068
01:34:50.130 --> 01:34:55.230
Martin Luther King talked to me all night.

1069
01:34:55.230 --> 01:35:09.254
He wanted us to make a difference, to get an education, get into a field that where you know they need help.

1070
01:35:09.254 --> 01:35:25.828
Because you are a victim of the brutality that puts you with the badge and that gun with authority to do what they did to you,

1071
01:35:25.828 --> 01:35:32.840
that you could prevent them from doing it to somebody else.

1072
01:35:32.840 --> 01:35:43.677
I couldn’t wait the next day to go talk to my CO about going to this school.

1073
01:35:43.677 --> 01:35:46.864
So I became a cop.

1074
01:35:46.864 --> 01:35:51.964
But I wasn’t [ . . . ?], I was a federal investigator.

1075
01:35:51.964 --> 01:36:01.526
So it’s just, that’s one of the things that they say happened for a reason.

1076
01:36:01.526 --> 01:36:07.263
Because I had no business going into the army.

1077
01:36:07.263 --> 01:36:08.538
The military.

1078
01:36:08.538 --> 01:36:13.000
Some things are beyond your control.

1079
01:36:13.000 --> 01:36:18.223
<v ARSENEAULT>What were some of your duties in Mossville as resident sheriff?

1080
01:36:18.223 --> 01:36:20.597
<v LEE>Well, I’d keep the peace.

1081
01:36:20.597 --> 01:36:24.871
I’d patrol and I would serve papers, civil papers.

1082
01:36:24.871 --> 01:36:26.295
I worked warrants.

1083
01:36:26.295 --> 01:36:31.993
The regular, just, that was my area that I took care of.

1084
01:36:31.993 --> 01:36:33.417
I worked traffic.

1085
01:36:33.417 --> 01:36:37.691
I gave tickets and I’d talk with the juveniles.

1086
01:36:37.691 --> 01:36:41.014
I kind of handled it my way.

1087
01:36:41.014 --> 01:36:52.410
And a lot of times I knew kids were doing certain things, I wouldn’t talk to the kid, I’d go talk to his dad.

1088
01:36:52.410 --> 01:37:01.906
I’d say, “Now, if you can’t stop him from doing what he did, I know he took Mr. Williams’ lawnmower.

1089
01:37:01.906 --> 01:37:02.856
You understand?

1090
01:37:02.856 --> 01:37:09.978
Now Mr. Williams ain’t got his lawnmower back by tomorrow, a certain time . . . in fact,

1091
01:37:09.978 --> 01:37:16.151
no. I want to see him cutting Mr. Williams’ yard with that lawnmower.”

1092
01:37:16.151 --> 01:37:19.000
You know what I’m saying?

1093
01:37:19.000 --> 01:37:20.548
<v ARSENEAULT>You gave him a chance.

1094
01:37:20.548 --> 01:37:20.858
<v LEE>Yeah.

1095
01:37:20.858 --> 01:37:22.406
Give him a second chance.

1096
01:37:22.406 --> 01:37:26.431
Now I say, “Now if you can’t do nothing about it, I can.

1097
01:37:26.431 --> 01:37:27.979
But I don’t want to.”

1098
01:37:27.979 --> 01:37:30.146
I said, “Maybe he’ll learn from this.”

1099
01:37:30.146 --> 01:37:35.720
Now a lot of times I went back over there and hey, he was cutting Mr. Williams’ yard.

1100
01:37:35.720 --> 01:37:37.577
And Mr. Williams go, “Hey, Kenny!”

1101
01:37:37.577 --> 01:37:43.151
I say, “Hey, man! [?] he’d take your lawnmower to the . . . and then cut his daddy’s and them yard,

1102
01:37:43.151 --> 01:37:45.628
then he’d come back and cut your yard.

1103
01:37:45.628 --> 01:37:49.653
Say he cut your yard before for you, he didn’t use your lawnmower.”

1104
01:37:49.653 --> 01:37:53.059
He said, “Yeah, he sure did, he cut my yard before.”

1105
01:37:53.059 --> 01:37:55.536
And I said, “That’s where your lawnmower was.

1106
01:37:55.536 --> 01:38:02.657
He had got your . . . used your lawnmower, knew you wouldn’t mind him using your lawnmower, because he’d come cut your grass for you.”

1107
01:38:02.657 --> 01:38:02.967
Yeah.

1108
01:38:02.967 --> 01:38:06.063
I say, “Yeah, but he was just sizing [him up?].”

1109
01:38:06.063 --> 01:38:13.184
I say, “Now you was just sizing Mr. Williams up to steal the lawnmower, just to find out where everything was around there.

1110
01:38:13.184 --> 01:38:16.900
Now if something else come up missing, I know you did it.

1111
01:38:16.900 --> 01:38:22.473
So if you didn’t do it and something else come missing, you better know who did, all right?”

1112
01:38:22.473 --> 01:38:25.259
I said, “I might let this go this time.”

1113
01:38:25.259 --> 01:38:28.975
But I said, “See how easy it is to get in trouble?”

1114
01:38:28.975 --> 01:38:33.000
And a lot of times, a lot of them learned from it.

1115
01:38:33.000 --> 01:38:35.609
<v ARSENEAULT>Were there any unsolved mysteries or anything?

1116
01:38:35.609 --> 01:38:39.709
<v LEE>No. Most of the stuff . . . They had break-ins at the school.

1117
01:38:39.709 --> 01:38:41.200
They would tell me.

1118
01:38:41.200 --> 01:38:49.773
They would call me and tell me, “So and so stole the lawnmower from the school,” or they did this, they done that.

1119
01:38:49.773 --> 01:38:59.091
And it just, I’d go to them and say, “Look, if that lawnmower ain’t back to that school in the morning when that bell ring.”

1120
01:38:59.091 --> 01:39:02.073
I say, “I should take you to jail.

1121
01:39:02.073 --> 01:39:05.055
But, you stealing from somebody ain’t got nothing.”

1122
01:39:05.055 --> 01:39:08.782
And I say, “Now, you’re going to make some mistakes.

1123
01:39:08.782 --> 01:39:11.018
You’re going to do some wrong.”

1124
01:39:11.018 --> 01:39:12.882
I say, “I’m not perfect.”

1125
01:39:12.882 --> 01:39:16.609
And I say, “I don’t expect you to be perfect.

1126
01:39:16.609 --> 01:39:20.709
But I expect you to try to correct what you’re doing.

1127
01:39:20.709 --> 01:39:26.300
You’re stealing from somebody that’s on a fixed income, or not a fixed up income.”

1128
01:39:26.300 --> 01:39:35.245
I say, “Now these people don’t have the money and stuff to replace these things that you all are doing this and that with.

1129
01:39:35.245 --> 01:39:39.345
Now go get that and bring that back to them people.”

1130
01:39:39.345 --> 01:39:43.445
I say, “I’m going to have to take you to jail.”

1131
01:39:43.445 --> 01:39:52.764
And I say, “I don’t want to go pull up in the front of your grandma house in the police car to pick you up.”

1132
01:39:52.764 --> 01:39:55.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You’re going to be embarrassed.

1133
01:39:55.000 --> 01:39:57.036
<v LEE>You’re going to embarrass your grandma.

1134
01:39:57.036 --> 01:39:58.732
You going to be embarrassed.

1135
01:39:58.732 --> 01:40:02.804
And I say, “Now I’m going to be halfway to being embarrassed.

1136
01:40:02.804 --> 01:40:06.196
But I don’t mind doing it, because that’s my job.

1137
01:40:06.196 --> 01:40:07.893
I’m going to do it.

1138
01:40:07.893 --> 01:40:12.982
But for your grandma’s sake, and something like that, I don’t want to do it.

1139
01:40:12.982 --> 01:40:15.357
So do what you’re supposed to do.

1140
01:40:15.357 --> 01:40:19.089
And you ain’t going to have that trouble out of me.”

1141
01:40:19.089 --> 01:40:20.786
You know what I’m saying?

1142
01:40:20.786 --> 01:40:27.571
Now most people would just run over there and kick your door in and do what they need to do.

1143
01:40:27.571 --> 01:40:31.643
But I’m going to try to work this out the community way.

1144
01:40:31.643 --> 01:40:37.750
And I say, “Now if you see something going on wrong that you think I need to know,

1145
01:40:37.750 --> 01:40:41.821
you call me and tell me, or come by and tell me.

1146
01:40:41.821 --> 01:40:44.875
If you don’t want to be seen, call me.

1147
01:40:44.875 --> 01:40:47.250
Here’s my card, here my phone number.”

1148
01:40:47.250 --> 01:40:52.000
And you would be surprised of the things that would come to you.

1149
01:40:52.000 --> 01:40:57.948
They had a kid from Mossville that took place, a murder in Houston.

1150
01:40:57.948 --> 01:41:01.609
He didn’t do it, but he was there.

1151
01:41:01.609 --> 01:41:03.897
He was a material witness.

1152
01:41:03.897 --> 01:41:08.015
Well, he cut out on them and came home.

1153
01:41:08.015 --> 01:41:08.472
Okay.

1154
01:41:08.472 --> 01:41:10.760
Houston found out about it.

1155
01:41:10.760 --> 01:41:16.708
They called the sheriff’s department, sent two people over there to investigate him.

1156
01:41:16.708 --> 01:41:23.572
So they called me because his grandfather and parents and them lived in my area.

1157
01:41:23.572 --> 01:41:24.487
I’m off.

1158
01:41:24.487 --> 01:41:25.402
I’m barbecuing.

1159
01:41:25.402 --> 01:41:26.317
True story.

1160
01:41:26.317 --> 01:41:26.775
“Kenny.”

1161
01:41:26.775 --> 01:41:28.148
I say, “Yeah.”

1162
01:41:28.148 --> 01:41:29.520
“What you doing?”

1163
01:41:29.520 --> 01:41:31.351
I say, “I’m barbecuing.”

1164
01:41:31.351 --> 01:41:37.299
He say, “I got two detectives here from Houston for so and so.”

1165
01:41:37.299 --> 01:41:38.672
I say, “Yeah?”

1166
01:41:38.672 --> 01:41:43.247
He say, “We need . . . They need to pick him up.

1167
01:41:43.247 --> 01:41:48.280
They need to talk to him about a murder in Houston.”

1168
01:41:48.280 --> 01:41:50.111
So I say, “Okay.”

1169
01:41:50.111 --> 01:41:52.856
So I got off the phone.

1170
01:41:52.856 --> 01:41:55.601
It wasn’t far from my house.

1171
01:41:55.601 --> 01:41:57.889
So I went over there.

1172
01:41:57.889 --> 01:42:01.092
I didn’t go in the police car.

1173
01:42:01.092 --> 01:42:02.923
The boy wasn’t there.

1174
01:42:02.923 --> 01:42:08.413
He say, “He go on and take my wife to the store.”

1175
01:42:08.413 --> 01:42:12.074
I said, “Look, let me tell you what.

1176
01:42:12.074 --> 01:42:21.683
They got two detectives from Houston coming to interview your grandson about a murder that he was a witness in Houston.”

1177
01:42:21.683 --> 01:42:33.122
I say now, “I don’t want to come back over here in the police car with these detectives following me in another car from Houston.”

1178
01:42:33.122 --> 01:42:36.782
I say, “You a preacher in this area.”

1179
01:42:36.782 --> 01:42:40.443
I say, “That ain’t going to look good.”

1180
01:42:40.443 --> 01:42:43.188
He said, “Kenny, I appreciate it.”

1181
01:42:43.188 --> 01:42:45.934
I say, “I tell you what.

1182
01:42:45.934 --> 01:42:51.882
When he come back with your wife, you bring him to my house.”

1183
01:42:51.882 --> 01:42:56.000
He say, “You got my word on that.”

1184
01:42:56.000 --> 01:42:59.156
So I went back home and start barbecuing.

1185
01:42:59.156 --> 01:43:00.340
I was cooking.

1186
01:43:00.340 --> 01:43:02.707
Here come the policemen from Houston.

1187
01:43:02.707 --> 01:43:03.891
They came by.

1188
01:43:03.891 --> 01:43:07.048
So they say, “We need so and so.”

1189
01:43:07.048 --> 01:43:10.204
I say, “Hey,” I say, “have a seat.

1190
01:43:10.204 --> 01:43:14.150
You want a soda or water or something like that?”

1191
01:43:14.150 --> 01:43:16.517
I say, “He’s on his way.

1192
01:43:16.517 --> 01:43:17.306
He’s coming.”

1193
01:43:17.306 --> 01:43:19.279
So he said, “Hey, man.

1194
01:43:19.279 --> 01:43:23.619
We need to get this done and get back to Houston.”

1195
01:43:23.619 --> 01:43:25.197
I say, “Hold on.

1196
01:43:25.197 --> 01:43:28.748
You’re in my town and you need my help.

1197
01:43:28.748 --> 01:43:29.143
Okay?

1198
01:43:29.143 --> 01:43:30.327
I’ve got this.

1199
01:43:30.327 --> 01:43:31.905
This is my town.

1200
01:43:31.905 --> 01:43:38.612
We might not do it like you do it in Houston, but we going to do it.”

1201
01:43:38.612 --> 01:43:46.109
I said, “Now, if you want me to help you, you help me by not getting on my nerve.

1202
01:43:46.109 --> 01:43:46.898
You understand?”

1203
01:43:46.898 --> 01:43:51.633
I say, “Because I’m going to pick the man up for you.

1204
01:43:51.633 --> 01:43:54.000
You’re going to see him.”

1205
01:43:54.000 --> 01:43:55.748
So my phone rang.

1206
01:43:55.748 --> 01:43:57.496
I answer the phone.

1207
01:43:57.496 --> 01:43:58.807
He say, “Kenny.”

1208
01:43:58.807 --> 01:44:00.992
I say, “What’s up, [Rev?]?”

1209
01:44:00.992 --> 01:44:03.613
He say, “We on our way.”

1210
01:44:03.613 --> 01:44:04.924
I say, “Good.”

1211
01:44:04.924 --> 01:44:08.857
I say, “He be here in about ten minutes.”

1212
01:44:08.857 --> 01:44:11.479
One of them says, “Like hell.

1213
01:44:11.479 --> 01:44:20.218
You going mean to tell me he going to come bring himself over here and turn himself in to you?

1214
01:44:20.218 --> 01:44:22.403
And he ran from Houston?”

1215
01:44:22.403 --> 01:44:24.588
I say, “Chill out, man.

1216
01:44:24.588 --> 01:44:28.084
I told you, this is my town, okay?”

1217
01:44:28.084 --> 01:44:29.832
So he sat down.

1218
01:44:29.832 --> 01:44:32.891
A few minutes, a car pull up.

1219
01:44:32.891 --> 01:44:34.202
Grandpa pull up.

1220
01:44:34.202 --> 01:44:35.950
He say, “Hey, you.”

1221
01:44:35.950 --> 01:44:38.134
The cop looked at me.

1222
01:44:38.134 --> 01:44:42.067
He say, “What kind of police stuff is this?

1223
01:44:42.067 --> 01:44:48.185
You mean to tell me you can get his grandpa to turn him in?”

1224
01:44:48.185 --> 01:44:53.429
I said, “Well, man, that’s depending on how you run your town.”

1225
01:44:53.429 --> 01:44:56.487
I say, “You run yours you way.

1226
01:44:56.487 --> 01:44:58.672
I run mine this way.”

1227
01:44:58.672 --> 01:45:02.168
And I said, “People got faith in me.

1228
01:45:02.168 --> 01:45:03.479
They trust me.

1229
01:45:03.479 --> 01:45:04.790
They know me.”

1230
01:45:04.790 --> 01:45:14.840
And I said, “Now you want to take him in, or you want to take him in my house and talk to him?

1231
01:45:14.840 --> 01:45:19.210
Or you got enough to take him downtown or whatever?”

1232
01:45:19.210 --> 01:45:22.706
And the other just kept shaking his head.

1233
01:45:22.706 --> 01:45:26.202
He said, “We can talk to him here.

1234
01:45:26.202 --> 01:45:29.697
We don’t need to really bring him in.

1235
01:45:29.697 --> 01:45:33.630
We just need a statement from him” and everything.

1236
01:45:33.630 --> 01:45:39.311
So they went in the house and they did the statement and everything.

1237
01:45:39.311 --> 01:45:50.672
Make a long story short, and they came back to me, they said, “Man, I ain’t never seen that kind of police shit in my life.”

1238
01:45:50.672 --> 01:45:57.227
I said, “Well, it depend on how you do it, how you run your town.”

1239
01:45:57.227 --> 01:46:05.529
They left, and about a week later, I got a letter from their officer, I guess, their superior officer.

1240
01:46:05.529 --> 01:46:09.899
They went back and told him what happened and everything.

1241
01:46:09.899 --> 01:46:17.328
And they said, man, they called the sheriff and told the sheriff the way I handled it.

1242
01:46:17.328 --> 01:46:20.824
And the sheriff say, “Well yeah, that’s Lee.

1243
01:46:20.824 --> 01:46:24.756
He got some strange ways, but they’re very effective.”

1244
01:46:24.756 --> 01:46:27.378
He said, “We call him Killer.”

1245
01:46:27.378 --> 01:46:30.000
He said, “That’s his nickname.”

1246
01:46:30.000 --> 01:46:30.378
<v ARSENEAULT>Killer?

1247
01:46:30.378 --> 01:46:30.756
<v LEE>Killer.

1248
01:46:30.756 --> 01:46:37.933
Because we got into a couple of fights and one of the fellows said, “Man, what happened to you?”

1249
01:46:37.933 --> 01:46:40.200
He said, “That fellow right there.

1250
01:46:40.200 --> 01:46:43.600
You all got that killer working for you all!

1251
01:46:43.600 --> 01:46:45.867
He just try to kill people.”

1252
01:46:45.867 --> 01:46:49.267
I say, “No.” I say, “You started the fight.”

1253
01:46:49.267 --> 01:46:52.289
I say, “I didn’t want to fight you.

1254
01:46:52.289 --> 01:46:55.689
I wanted to bring you in, do my job.

1255
01:46:55.689 --> 01:46:57.956
But it’s like I told you.

1256
01:46:57.956 --> 01:47:01.733
Anytime,” I say, “you want me to call an ambulance?

1257
01:47:01.733 --> 01:47:02.867
Or you want [ . . . ?].

1258
01:47:02.867 --> 01:47:05.511
Because I ain’t going to play fight.

1259
01:47:05.511 --> 01:47:08.156
I’m going to fight you to win.

1260
01:47:08.156 --> 01:47:13.444
If I’ve got to cheat on you, I’m going to fight you to win.”

1261
01:47:13.444 --> 01:47:15.711
And he say, “You a killer?”

1262
01:47:15.711 --> 01:47:19.111
And ever since then, they’ve been calling me Killer.

1263
01:47:19.111 --> 01:47:21.000
They’ve been calling me Killer.

1264
01:47:21.000 --> 01:47:26.289
They called me Killer the whole while I worked there at the sheriff’s office.

1265
01:47:26.289 --> 01:47:29.689
Half the time, I wouldn’t even have a gun.

1266
01:47:29.689 --> 01:47:31.956
I’d have it in my pocket.

1267
01:47:31.956 --> 01:47:33.467
I wouldn’t wear it.

1268
01:47:33.467 --> 01:47:38.000
I’d go somewhere and knew everybody, because I played ball here.

1269
01:47:38.000 --> 01:47:43.045
I stayed there in Mossville, and I’d give them a fair break.

1270
01:47:43.045 --> 01:47:45.146
“You done broke the law.

1271
01:47:45.146 --> 01:47:50.611
I’m going to have to do this, or you need to do this,

1272
01:47:50.611 --> 01:47:59.439
or give me them keys and I’ll pull your car over there and take you home and tell your wife where,

1273
01:47:59.439 --> 01:48:03.643
is your wife home, where the car at and everything.”

1274
01:48:03.643 --> 01:48:07.427
I say, “Because I can take you to jail.

1275
01:48:07.427 --> 01:48:12.892
It’s going to cost you some money, and you might lose your job.”

1276
01:48:12.892 --> 01:48:17.096
I say, “You’re not really drunk, but you’ve been drinking.

1277
01:48:17.096 --> 01:48:20.038
I can smell the alcohol on you.

1278
01:48:20.038 --> 01:48:23.401
I’ve got probable cause to take you in.

1279
01:48:23.401 --> 01:48:25.503
But you ain’t hit nobody.

1280
01:48:25.503 --> 01:48:28.025
Pull that car over right there.

1281
01:48:28.025 --> 01:48:29.707
Leave that car there.

1282
01:48:29.707 --> 01:48:35.592
And I’m going to take your keys and I’m going to take you home.

1283
01:48:35.592 --> 01:48:40.637
And I’m going to give your keys to somebody at your house.

1284
01:48:40.637 --> 01:48:44.000
And if there ain’t nobody at your house,

1285
01:48:44.000 --> 01:48:54.089
I’m going to keep the keys and come back later and give them to somebody to keep you from going to get that car.”

1286
01:48:54.089 --> 01:48:56.611
A lot of times, it worked.

1287
01:48:56.611 --> 01:49:01.656
Later on I’d see them and they’d say, “I appreciate that, man.”

1288
01:49:01.656 --> 01:49:03.338
And they’d call me.

1289
01:49:03.338 --> 01:49:07.541
They’d see something happening, they’d call me and tell me.

1290
01:49:07.541 --> 01:49:16.369
They’d say, “Because you gave me a chance, that’s going on over here, and we need this, that and the other.”

1291
01:49:16.369 --> 01:49:20.153
Or, “So and so, I seen him do this.

1292
01:49:20.153 --> 01:49:22.255
I seen him do that.”

1293
01:49:22.255 --> 01:49:24.777
But one hand washed the other.

1294
01:49:24.777 --> 01:49:26.879
And nothing major had happened.

1295
01:49:26.879 --> 01:49:30.662
Well back then, you could do things like that.

1296
01:49:30.662 --> 01:49:36.968
In the ‘70s and ‘80s, and the early ‘90s, you could kind of control things.

1297
01:49:36.968 --> 01:49:39.490
You knew what was going on.

1298
01:49:39.490 --> 01:49:46.637
Because like the paper boy knew more about what’s going on in your town than the mayor.

1299
01:49:46.637 --> 01:49:51.682
Because when the mayor’s sleeping, the paper boy out making his rounds.

1300
01:49:51.682 --> 01:49:55.045
There are certain people you’d stay tight with.

1301
01:49:55.045 --> 01:50:02.191
And them old ladies, them clubs and stuff they had, them organizations, they know what’s going on.

1302
01:50:02.191 --> 01:50:04.713
The paperboy know what’s going on.

1303
01:50:04.713 --> 01:50:07.656
The mailman knew what was going on.

1304
01:50:07.656 --> 01:50:14.803
Because I was a mailman for seven years, and you see all kind of stuff going on.

1305
01:50:14.803 --> 01:50:19.427
So all these people feed you the information that you need.

1306
01:50:19.427 --> 01:50:26.573
And they see stuff, and they wouldn’t hesitate about calling to tell me what was going on,

1307
01:50:26.573 --> 01:50:31.197
where it was going on, or why it was going on.

1308
01:50:31.197 --> 01:50:40.446
They’d never hear it, but I was working on a way that it’s like it got to me that you doing this.

1309
01:50:40.446 --> 01:50:42.548
Now, “Is you doing this?”

1310
01:50:42.548 --> 01:50:51.796
“Oh, no, you wouldn’t do that, because you know I’m going to get you.” [whistles] It’s nothing major happening to the place,

1311
01:50:51.796 --> 01:50:56.000
but you’re not doing what you’re supposed to do.

1312
01:50:56.000 --> 01:51:00.061
<v ARSENEAULT>You got it before it got out of hand.

1313
01:51:00.061 --> 01:51:00.512
<v LEE>Yeah.

1314
01:51:00.512 --> 01:51:03.220
You’ve got to control the situation.

1315
01:51:03.220 --> 01:51:07.280
He’d say, “Well, okay, that’s the way it is.”

1316
01:51:07.280 --> 01:51:09.988
But that was Mossville for you.

1317
01:51:09.988 --> 01:51:12.695
A lot of people appreciated me.

1318
01:51:12.695 --> 01:51:19.012
And then people that didn’t live in Mossville, you’d drive up on somebody, three,

1319
01:51:19.012 --> 01:51:25.329
four o’clock in the morning, they’re sitting there waiting for their train to go.

1320
01:51:25.329 --> 01:51:27.585
Then they went to sleep.

1321
01:51:27.585 --> 01:51:29.841
Sitting at the train tracks.

1322
01:51:29.841 --> 01:51:33.000
<v ARSENEAULT>[laughs] That almost happened to me.

1323
01:51:33.000 --> 01:51:33.314
<v LEE>Yeah.

1324
01:51:33.314 --> 01:51:35.824
They’d call me and tell me, “Hey, Kenny.

1325
01:51:35.824 --> 01:51:39.902
They got somebody sleeping in the car down there at the railroad track.

1326
01:51:39.902 --> 01:51:42.725
I didn’t know if they was drunk or died.

1327
01:51:42.725 --> 01:51:45.549
I didn’t want to stop and check them out.

1328
01:51:45.549 --> 01:51:49.314
You better go check them out, see what’s going on with them.”

1329
01:51:49.314 --> 01:51:52.451
And there’s people there that I had done favors for.

1330
01:51:52.451 --> 01:51:54.647
Not necessarily favors, but that they would,

1331
01:51:54.647 --> 01:52:00.922
they felt like they owed me something because I had kind of helped them out, or helped their nephew out.

1332
01:52:00.922 --> 01:52:03.431
Or I’m going to take you to jail.

1333
01:52:03.431 --> 01:52:04.059
You’re hungry?

1334
01:52:04.059 --> 01:52:11.902
I’m going to pass and get you a piece of chicken or something before I take you to jail because they already ate at jail.

1335
01:52:11.902 --> 01:52:14.098
And I’ve got to take you downtown.

1336
01:52:14.098 --> 01:52:20.373
But if you’re hungry, I’m going to take you and get you something to eat before you go to jail.

1337
01:52:20.373 --> 01:52:25.392
Okay, now if there’s something in that jail going on that you think I should know,

1338
01:52:25.392 --> 01:52:29.471
or anything you see in the future that you think I should know,

1339
01:52:29.471 --> 01:52:33.863
when you come of jail, you ain’t doing that stuff, what you doing now,

1340
01:52:33.863 --> 01:52:38.569
no more, because you’re going to the hotel, you’re going to join the big boy.

1341
01:52:38.569 --> 01:52:43.275
I’m going to talk to him, try to get him to put you into trustee.

1342
01:52:43.275 --> 01:52:51.431
But if you know of anything or hear of anything going on in this jail or anywhere in the future that you think I should know,

1343
01:52:51.431 --> 01:52:53.000
I want to know.

1344
01:52:53.000 --> 01:52:57.422
And I’m not going to implicate you as the person that told me.

1345
01:52:57.422 --> 01:53:01.503
I said, I’m going to work it just like I did you.

1346
01:53:01.503 --> 01:53:04.905
Because somebody told me what you was doing, you understand?

1347
01:53:04.905 --> 01:53:08.986
But I got to do what I got to do, you understand?

1348
01:53:08.986 --> 01:53:11.707
But I’m going to try to help you.

1349
01:53:11.707 --> 01:53:16.469
I’m going to get them to put you where the trustees at or something.

1350
01:53:16.469 --> 01:53:18.170
You know what I’m saying?

1351
01:53:18.170 --> 01:53:20.211
Throw something out there for you.

1352
01:53:20.211 --> 01:53:25.993
And if you need something on your book, those people don’t come put nothing on your book,

1353
01:53:25.993 --> 01:53:30.415
I’m going to put ten, twelve dollars on your book for your commissary.

1354
01:53:30.415 --> 01:53:32.116
You know what I’m saying?

1355
01:53:32.116 --> 01:53:35.517
I say, “Because I’ve been knowing you all your life.”

1356
01:53:35.517 --> 01:53:41.299
You’d be surprised at what the things that would come back to you from just doing that.

1357
01:53:41.299 --> 01:53:44.701
Not exactly that, or something similar to that for them.

1358
01:53:44.701 --> 01:53:46.401
You know what I’m saying?

1359
01:53:46.401 --> 01:53:52.524
Even though sometimes they say, “Well, man, I’m not hungry, I already ate, or this, that, the other.”

1360
01:53:52.524 --> 01:53:58.646
After we got the cell phone, “Somebody else you need to call before we get to the jail?

1361
01:53:58.646 --> 01:54:01.367
I’ll let you use my phone to call.

1362
01:54:01.367 --> 01:54:06.129
Because you get to jail, you ain’t going to get but one phone call.

1363
01:54:06.129 --> 01:54:14.293
Want to call your girlfriend or your wife or whoever and tell her, hey, you’re going to jail, or this, that and the other?

1364
01:54:14.293 --> 01:54:18.034
Because there’s always somebody that needs to know where you’re at.”

1365
01:54:18.034 --> 01:54:21.776
I say, “You can use my phone before we get there.

1366
01:54:21.776 --> 01:54:28.238
Or when we get there, before we get in, I lock you up, I’ll let you use my phone.”

1367
01:54:28.238 --> 01:54:33.000
And man, you’d be surprised at stuff that would come back to you.

1368
01:54:33.000 --> 01:54:34.708
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of stuff?

1369
01:54:34.708 --> 01:54:37.270
If you don’t mind me asking.

1370
01:54:37.270 --> 01:54:40.685
<v LEE>I don’t want to go in no detail.

1371
01:54:40.685 --> 01:54:43.674
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah, you’re implicating a lot of people.

1372
01:54:43.674 --> 01:54:49.225
<v LEE>But some of it was major and some of it was minor stuff.

1373
01:54:49.225 --> 01:54:51.360
And they would tell you.

1374
01:54:51.360 --> 01:54:56.910
They got a car in the river up there at the boat launch.

1375
01:54:56.910 --> 01:54:58.618
<v ARSENEAULT>Was somebody in it?

1376
01:54:58.618 --> 01:55:00.326
<v LEE>No, nobody in it.

1377
01:55:00.326 --> 01:55:01.180
A car.

1378
01:55:01.180 --> 01:55:11.427
Somebody stole it and, so and so stole it and joyriding, and when they got through, they went and put it in the river.

1379
01:55:11.427 --> 01:55:19.966
And then you go, you call them people, they get the diving, then they go up there and they check.

1380
01:55:19.966 --> 01:55:23.382
Sure enough, there’s a car in the river.

1381
01:55:23.382 --> 01:55:24.663
Stuff like that.

1382
01:55:24.663 --> 01:55:26.798
You know what I’m saying?

1383
01:55:26.798 --> 01:55:35.764
And they got a car they got stripped down up there at such and such a place and they stripped it,

1384
01:55:35.764 --> 01:55:38.753
at so and so’s place up there.

1385
01:55:38.753 --> 01:55:40.034
Stuff like that.

1386
01:55:40.034 --> 01:55:43.022
It paid off in the long run.

1387
01:55:43.022 --> 01:55:46.865
But after I retired, it got out of hand.

1388
01:55:46.865 --> 01:55:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you mean?

1389
01:55:49.000 --> 01:55:51.747
<v LEE>I don’t want to throw no rocks.

1390
01:55:51.747 --> 01:56:01.560
It was good while I was there, and I felt like I made a big difference in the community, in the way things got along.

1391
01:56:01.560 --> 01:56:14.119
There were times that deputies went to arrest somebody over there and they was boxed in and it was a miscommunication that yesterday I was telling my old lady the other night,

1392
01:56:14.119 --> 01:56:16.474
how we be seeing this fellow.

1393
01:56:16.474 --> 01:56:19.614
Well, him and his wife got into it.

1394
01:56:19.614 --> 01:56:21.577
He worked on the railroad.

1395
01:56:21.577 --> 01:56:23.147
They got into it.

1396
01:56:23.147 --> 01:56:29.427
And he, to make a long story short, she worked in the restaurant for her brother.

1397
01:56:29.427 --> 01:56:35.314
They lived behind the restaurant, in the alley and him and her got into it.

1398
01:56:35.314 --> 01:56:37.276
So she called the police.

1399
01:56:37.276 --> 01:56:39.631
They asked her did, what happened.

1400
01:56:39.631 --> 01:56:42.379
And they say they got into it.

1401
01:56:42.379 --> 01:56:51.406
And they asked her did he have a gun in the house, and she said, “Yeah, he got a gun in the house.”

1402
01:56:51.406 --> 01:56:58.471
Well, they thought that she had, that he had the gun on for her the way he’d got.

1403
01:56:58.471 --> 01:57:03.966
So they went and knocked on the door and they say, “Who is it?”

1404
01:57:03.966 --> 01:57:05.536
They say, “Sheriff Department.”

1405
01:57:05.536 --> 01:57:07.891
He said, “Is that Kenny Lee?”

1406
01:57:07.891 --> 01:57:10.638
They say, “No, it’s not Kenny Lee.

1407
01:57:10.638 --> 01:57:12.208
It’s so and so.”

1408
01:57:12.208 --> 01:57:14.956
“I ain’t going to jail for nobody.

1409
01:57:14.956 --> 01:57:20.451
Anybody take me to jail, Kenny Lee going to come take me to jail.”

1410
01:57:20.451 --> 01:57:26.338
He said, “If I need to go to jail, Kenny Lee going to bring me.”

1411
01:57:26.338 --> 01:57:29.870
They say, “Well, you got a gun in there?”

1412
01:57:29.870 --> 01:57:33.010
“Yeah, I got a gun in my house.”

1413
01:57:33.010 --> 01:57:35.758
He ain’t had the gun for them.

1414
01:57:35.758 --> 01:57:37.720
The way they asked him.

1415
01:57:37.720 --> 01:57:42.430
And she had told them, “He got a gun in the house.”

1416
01:57:42.430 --> 01:57:44.000
So they thought . . .

1417
01:57:44.000 --> 01:57:46.395
<v ARSENEAULT>He was about to shoot somebody.

1418
01:57:46.395 --> 01:57:46.795
<v LEE>Yeah.

1419
01:57:46.795 --> 01:57:49.190
He was going to shoot somebody.

1420
01:57:49.190 --> 01:57:53.582
So to make a long story short, so they called me.

1421
01:57:53.582 --> 01:57:55.977
And I happened to be home.

1422
01:57:55.977 --> 01:57:59.570
They say, “Kenny, do you know so and so?”

1423
01:57:59.570 --> 01:58:00.768
I say, “Yeah.”

1424
01:58:00.768 --> 01:58:04.760
He say, “Well anyway, he’s in the house down here.

1425
01:58:04.760 --> 01:58:08.354
And him and his wife have got into it.

1426
01:58:08.354 --> 01:58:15.141
And it came down to trouble, he said he wouldn’t go into jail with nobody but you.

1427
01:58:15.141 --> 01:58:18.734
You the only one could take him to jail.

1428
01:58:18.734 --> 01:58:23.525
They say he’s a pretty big man, he worked on the railroad.”

1429
01:58:23.525 --> 01:58:26.719
I said, “Yeah, he is a big fellow.”

1430
01:58:26.719 --> 01:58:29.913
I say, “He’s a pretty good man, too.”

1431
01:58:29.913 --> 01:58:32.707
I say, “Tell them to hold off.

1432
01:58:32.707 --> 01:58:33.506
I’m coming.

1433
01:58:33.506 --> 01:58:34.304
I’ll go.”

1434
01:58:34.304 --> 01:58:35.901
So I get there.

1435
01:58:35.901 --> 01:58:36.700
I go.

1436
01:58:36.700 --> 01:58:45.882
I got out, I walked up to the door and I say, I called his name and I said, “Open this damn door.”

1437
01:58:45.882 --> 01:58:48.278
He say, “Kenny, is that you?”

1438
01:58:48.278 --> 01:58:50.274
I say, “Yeah, that’s me.”

1439
01:58:50.274 --> 01:58:53.468
I say, “Why you driving me down here?

1440
01:58:53.468 --> 01:58:55.464
I’m about to eat supper.”

1441
01:58:55.464 --> 01:59:01.852
Yeah, I got a shotgun in here for hunting,’ ‘Yeah, I got a gun in here.’

1442
01:59:01.852 --> 01:59:04.646
Like you got a gun on somebody.”

1443
01:59:04.646 --> 01:59:09.837
I say, “You ain’t going to bust a [cap?] in a food fight.”

1444
01:59:09.837 --> 01:59:12.232
He say, “Man, you know that.”

1445
01:59:12.232 --> 01:59:17.023
But he say, “I didn’t want those fellows coming up in here,

1446
01:59:17.023 --> 01:59:26.205
taking me to jail when I can go with you.” [laughter] I say, “Damn, “I said, “don’t you do that no more, man!

1447
01:59:26.205 --> 01:59:29.000
You could have got killed, boy!”

1448
01:59:29.000 --> 01:59:30.089
<v ARSENEAULT>[laughs] That’s funny.

1449
01:59:30.089 --> 01:59:34.447
He was mad at his wife because she didn’t cook him dinner.

1450
01:59:34.447 --> 01:59:34.810
<v LEE>Yeah.

1451
01:59:34.810 --> 01:59:36.626
“You mad over a lunch!

1452
01:59:36.626 --> 01:59:42.436
You could have went up there to your brother-in-law’s place and got you something to eat!”

1453
01:59:42.436 --> 01:59:45.704
He said, “Well, she could have brought it home.”

1454
01:59:45.704 --> 01:59:50.788
I said, “Damn, now this ain’t . . .” I said, “Well anyway, don’t worry about it.”

1455
01:59:50.788 --> 01:59:51.877
Things like that.

1456
01:59:51.877 --> 01:59:56.598
He say, “Well, I don’t want to go along with nobody but you.

1457
01:59:56.598 --> 01:59:59.866
Because I know you’re going to treat me right.

1458
01:59:59.866 --> 02:00:06.765
Because didn’t you take so and so and got him something to eat before you took him to jail?”

1459
02:00:06.765 --> 02:00:07.855
I said, “Yeah.”

1460
02:00:07.855 --> 02:00:14.028
He said, “Well, I know them boys weren’t going to take me to get nothing to eat.

1461
02:00:14.028 --> 02:00:20.201
You would have brought me and got me something to eat before you took me to jail.”

1462
02:00:20.201 --> 02:00:23.832
I said lord, you’re rubbing me the wrong way, boy.

1463
02:00:23.832 --> 02:00:27.101
He said, “Well you would have, man, wouldn’t you?”

1464
02:00:27.101 --> 02:00:29.279
I said “Yeah, I would have”.

1465
02:00:29.279 --> 02:00:31.095
We went to school together.

1466
02:00:31.095 --> 02:00:34.000
But they took it the wrong way.

1467
02:00:34.000 --> 02:00:38.686
<v ARSENEAULT>Well, Mr. Kenny . . . We’re . . . Yeah, we got a good interview this time.

1468
02:00:38.686 --> 02:00:42.201
I didn’t get to ask all my questions, though.

1469
02:00:42.201 --> 02:00:45.325
So we might have to do one more.

1470
02:00:45.325 --> 02:00:46.497
<v LEE>What you want?

1471
02:00:46.497 --> 02:00:47.669
What you want?

1472
02:00:47.669 --> 02:00:52.746
<v ARSENEAULT>Well, I don’t want to take up too much of your time, and . . .

1473
02:00:52.746 --> 02:00:56.260
<v LEE>Oh, yeah, I get carried away talking about Mossville.

1474
02:00:56.260 --> 02:00:59.385
<v ARSENEAULT>No, well, it’s a lot of good stuff.

1475
02:00:59.385 --> 02:01:02.899
I guess maybe we’ll do one more next time.

1476
02:01:02.899 --> 02:01:11.101
I can talk to you about like, I didn’t get to all of the questions about the entertainment in the area.

1477
02:01:11.101 --> 02:01:17.349
I wanted to know about the zydeco, if there were any like local musicians or anything?

1478
02:01:17.349 --> 02:01:23.207
<v LEE>Well, zydeco wasn’t big back then. [Buzu?] at the Paradise, it wasn’t that much zydeco.

1479
02:01:23.207 --> 02:01:30.237
It was just mostly the big name entertainments and Mac Shepherd, Little Mac, it’s all the same people,

1480
02:01:30.237 --> 02:01:36.095
they would open for some of the big bands and stuff, big groups and everything.

1481
02:01:36.095 --> 02:01:38.047
<v ARSENEAULT>So those were local people?

1482
02:01:38.047 --> 02:01:40.000
Mack Sheppard was local?

1483
02:01:40.000 --> 02:01:40.477
<v LEE>Yeah.

1484
02:01:40.477 --> 02:01:42.383
Mack Sheppard was local.

1485
02:01:42.383 --> 02:01:44.290
He was the . . . Helen!

1486
02:01:44.290 --> 02:01:50.963
That was his nephew [Joshua Ledet] or great-nephew on The Voice, or American Idol.

1487
02:01:50.963 --> 02:01:51.916
American Idol.

1488
02:01:51.916 --> 02:01:52.869
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

1489
02:01:52.869 --> 02:01:53.346
Okay.

1490
02:01:53.346 --> 02:01:53.822
Okay.

1491
02:01:53.822 --> 02:01:56.682
<v LEE>He won that from this area.

1492
02:01:56.682 --> 02:02:01.925
He was in . . . I think he came out second or third.

1493
02:02:01.925 --> 02:02:06.692
But he was one of the top ones on there.

1494
02:02:06.692 --> 02:02:10.505
He stayed on there for a long time.

1495
02:02:10.505 --> 02:02:13.841
His daddy was a minister of Mossville.

1496
02:02:13.841 --> 02:02:14.794
<v ARSENEAULT>What church?

1497
02:02:14.794 --> 02:02:19.084
<v LEE>I think it was a . . . no, it wasn’t Philadelphia.

1498
02:02:19.084 --> 02:02:27.187
It was right there at the corner of Rigmaiden Avenue, we called it the Reverend Gasaway’s Church.

1499
02:02:27.187 --> 02:02:30.047
<v ARSENEAULT>That was his name, Reverend Galloway?

1500
02:02:30.047 --> 02:02:31.000
<v LEE>Gasaway.

1501
02:02:31.000 --> 02:02:31.686
<v ARSENEAULT>Gasaway.

1502
02:02:31.686 --> 02:02:39.229
<v LEE>His daddy was a Ledet [pronounced luh-day], a Ledet [pronounced luh-det].

1503
02:02:39.229 --> 02:02:39.914
Nathaniel.

1504
02:02:39.914 --> 02:02:41.286
Nathan Ledet.

1505
02:02:41.286 --> 02:02:41.971
Helen!

1506
02:02:41.971 --> 02:02:45.400
Well anyway, that boy was . . .

1507
02:02:45.400 --> 02:02:48.829
<v ARSENEAULT>What about the Cowboy Inn?

1508
02:02:48.829 --> 02:02:50.200
<v LEE>Cowboy Inn.

1509
02:02:50.200 --> 02:02:50.886
Yeah.

1510
02:02:50.886 --> 02:02:52.257
Peter Deville.

1511
02:02:52.257 --> 02:02:55.000
<v ARSENEAULT>He owned it?

1512
02:02:55.000 --> 02:02:55.522
<v LEE>Yeah.

1513
02:02:55.522 --> 02:02:57.088
He owned that.

1514
02:02:57.088 --> 02:02:59.175
Peter Deville ran that.

1515
02:02:59.175 --> 02:03:03.351
He didn’t have dances or nothing like that.

1516
02:03:03.351 --> 02:03:06.482
It was just the neighborhood bar.

1517
02:03:06.482 --> 02:03:11.180
The country club, John Williams and them owned that.

1518
02:03:11.180 --> 02:03:19.009
That’s the club I opened up later on, The Pub, was the old country club.

1519
02:03:19.009 --> 02:03:22.662
<v ARSENEAULT>Who would go to the Cowboy Inn?

1520
02:03:22.662 --> 02:03:23.184
<v LEE>Locals.

1521
02:03:23.184 --> 02:03:25.272
A lot of locals.

1522
02:03:25.272 --> 02:03:27.360
A lot of people.

1523
02:03:27.360 --> 02:03:31.535
And wasn’t no, it wasn’t like the Paradise.

1524
02:03:31.535 --> 02:03:35.711
It was just a pool hall and drinking.

1525
02:03:35.711 --> 02:03:37.798
Paradise had live entertainment.

1526
02:03:37.798 --> 02:03:40.408
You know what I’m saying?

1527
02:03:40.408 --> 02:03:44.583
It was more of a show place like.

1528
02:03:44.583 --> 02:03:48.759
But the Cowboy Inn was just a nightclub.

1529
02:03:48.759 --> 02:03:58.675
It had a lot of regulars that would go there and drink and shoot pool and stuff like that.

1530
02:03:58.675 --> 02:04:07.026
But the Paradise was, I’d say a different class because of the entertainment that they had.

1531
02:04:07.026 --> 02:04:10.158
The Cowboy Inn had good business.

1532
02:04:10.158 --> 02:04:12.246
It had good business.

1533
02:04:12.246 --> 02:04:17.465
The bar business was a good business in that area.

1534
02:04:17.465 --> 02:04:23.206
You had a lot of people that would come in there.

1535
02:04:23.206 --> 02:04:27.904
They had a little trouble every now and then.

1536
02:04:27.904 --> 02:04:37.298
But it wasn’t nothing that, you know, very seldom you had something that was, that required a police.

1537
02:04:37.298 --> 02:04:42.518
I believe they came to my club maybe one time.

1538
02:04:42.518 --> 02:04:46.171
But it wasn’t something at the club.

1539
02:04:46.171 --> 02:04:54.000
It was something that transpired on the street during the period I had it.

1540
02:04:54.000 --> 02:05:01.445
But the Cowboy Inn, it was kind of, it was what you call a Cowboy Inn.

1541
02:05:01.445 --> 02:05:05.633
They’d fight, and next fifteen minutes they drinking together.

1542
02:05:05.633 --> 02:05:14.939
Like I told them boys, “Just don’t rush to get there, unless there’s a gun involved or something like that.”

1543
02:05:14.939 --> 02:05:16.335
Take our time.

1544
02:05:16.335 --> 02:05:21.919
By the time I’ll get there, the fighting won’t last that long.

1545
02:05:21.919 --> 02:05:30.295
By the time the sheriff’s department get there, it was over and they sitting down there drinking together.

1546
02:05:30.295 --> 02:05:32.621
Whatever had happened had happened.

1547
02:05:32.621 --> 02:05:34.948
You know what I’m saying?

1548
02:05:34.948 --> 02:05:40.997
But you never know what it’s going to turn into until they do.

1549
02:05:40.997 --> 02:05:45.185
But they had a lot of disagreements and stuff.

1550
02:05:45.185 --> 02:05:47.046
But nothing hardly major.

1551
02:05:47.046 --> 02:05:54.491
You could count on the major things that happened at all these clubs, all of them,

1552
02:05:54.491 --> 02:06:01.006
in the period of time I was in law enforcement, because everybody knew everybody.

1553
02:06:01.006 --> 02:06:04.728
“I don’t want to press charges on him!”

1554
02:06:04.728 --> 02:06:07.520
And this, that and the other.

1555
02:06:07.520 --> 02:06:14.965
If you take, it took over fifteen minutes to get there sometimes from where you was.

1556
02:06:14.965 --> 02:06:24.272
If I wasn’t on duty, by the time they sent another unit in there, they’d get there, it’s over with.

1557
02:06:24.272 --> 02:06:27.064
They say oh, it’s a disturbance.

1558
02:06:27.064 --> 02:06:31.717
They’d just list the people that was involved in it.

1559
02:06:31.717 --> 02:06:33.113
And that’s it.

1560
02:06:33.113 --> 02:06:38.697
But on the up and up, compared club-wise, it was pretty low-key.

1561
02:06:38.697 --> 02:06:41.954
They had a fight at the Cowboy.

1562
02:06:41.954 --> 02:06:45.676
Well, she might have snapped at her husband.

1563
02:06:45.676 --> 02:06:53.121
Somebody came in there and her husband might have been sitting down talking with another woman.

1564
02:06:53.121 --> 02:06:54.517
A big fight.

1565
02:06:54.517 --> 02:06:55.913
That ain’t nothing.

1566
02:06:55.913 --> 02:06:58.240
You know what I’m saying?

1567
02:06:58.240 --> 02:07:03.824
A lot times, that was all conversation or somebody was being messy,

1568
02:07:03.824 --> 02:07:10.803
call so and so, and told her that her husband down there with some women.

1569
02:07:10.803 --> 02:07:21.040
And nine out of ten, that was his second cousin or first cousin that he’s sitting at the table talking with them.

1570
02:07:21.040 --> 02:07:22.436
Stuff like that.

1571
02:07:22.436 --> 02:07:23.367
Nothing major.

1572
02:07:23.367 --> 02:07:25.228
At the Cowboy Inn.

1573
02:07:25.228 --> 02:07:30.812
And they had another club, Marge’s, that was a little neighborhood bar.

1574
02:07:30.812 --> 02:07:32.208
And same thing.

1575
02:07:32.208 --> 02:07:33.139
No entertainment.

1576
02:07:33.139 --> 02:07:35.000
Nothing but jukebox.

1577
02:07:35.000 --> 02:07:38.205
<v ARSENEAULT>Where else did people go for live entertainment?

1578
02:07:38.205 --> 02:07:39.006
<v LEE>Not Mossville.

1579
02:07:39.006 --> 02:07:39.807
Lake Charles.

1580
02:07:39.807 --> 02:07:48.220
They have a lot of different, when I was coming up, some baseball activities and football games and stuff like that.

1581
02:07:48.220 --> 02:07:55.432
That was the major things, Friday night football and basketball games and stuff like that . . . and the club.

1582
02:07:55.432 --> 02:08:03.444
The only thing they had, that had live entertainment was the Paradise, and the people come from everywhere for that.

1583
02:08:03.444 --> 02:08:11.857
They’d be cars, like I said, all over Queensboro and down there, parked, and all the way from . . . all down highway,

1584
02:08:11.857 --> 02:08:21.472
Prater Road, all the way from Old Spanish Trail all the way to the— Just tickled me, I laughed as I thought about something.

1585
02:08:21.472 --> 02:08:27.081
They had a church on the corner of Prater Road and Old Spanish Trail,

1586
02:08:27.081 --> 02:08:33.090
and my grandmother and them lived in the next house right there behind the church,

1587
02:08:33.090 --> 02:08:38.298
on the other side of the church, going west on Old Spanish Trail.

1588
02:08:38.298 --> 02:08:46.711
They had those big dances and people would be parked all the way down there in the church yard and everything.

1589
02:08:46.711 --> 02:08:48.714
But Grandma would say, “Kenny!”

1590
02:08:48.714 --> 02:08:50.317
I was a kid.

1591
02:08:50.317 --> 02:09:00.733
“Go over there and make sure there ain’t nobody sleeping in the cars from last night, drunk.” [laughs] Before the people come down to have church.

1592
02:09:00.733 --> 02:09:07.143
I was a kid, and I’d go there and say, “So and so is over there.”

1593
02:09:07.143 --> 02:09:09.146
She’d say, “I figured that.

1594
02:09:09.146 --> 02:09:15.556
I seen that car over there and I figured they had been there since all night.

1595
02:09:15.556 --> 02:09:25.571
So you go over there and wake them up and tell them to move because the people are supposed to come have church over there.

1596
02:09:25.571 --> 02:09:28.376
They don’t want to be caught there.”

1597
02:09:28.376 --> 02:09:29.578
Stuff like that.

1598
02:09:29.578 --> 02:09:31.581
That was kind of comical.

1599
02:09:31.581 --> 02:09:32.783
She was policing.

1600
02:09:32.783 --> 02:09:39.994
I’d say, “See Grandma, I was the police before I knew I was going to be a police.”

1601
02:09:39.994 --> 02:09:41.596
She’s say, “Yeah, boy.”

1602
02:09:41.596 --> 02:09:44.000
She said, “You my boy.”

1603
02:09:44.000 --> 02:09:44.980
<v ARSENEAULT>She trained you.

1604
02:09:44.980 --> 02:09:45.306
<v LEE>[Pardon?]

1605
02:09:45.306 --> 02:09:46.286
<v ARSENEAULT>She trained you.

1606
02:09:46.286 --> 02:09:46.612
<v LEE>Yeah.

1607
02:09:46.612 --> 02:09:49.551
I said, “You get me started in my background.

1608
02:09:49.551 --> 02:09:55.102
Go over there and check people out and make sure they get them off the church yard.”

1609
02:09:55.102 --> 02:09:57.388
<v ARSENEAULT>They’d be embarrassed if they got caught.

1610
02:09:57.388 --> 02:09:59.020
<v LEE>Yeah, that’s what she said.

1611
02:09:59.020 --> 02:10:01.633
She said, “You wouldn’t want somebody to come.”

1612
02:10:01.633 --> 02:10:02.939
I said, “No, ma’am.”

1613
02:10:02.939 --> 02:10:05.878
She said, “Well go over there and make sure,

1614
02:10:05.878 --> 02:10:12.735
and if they got any beer bottles or beer cans over there, pick them up and bring them back over here.

1615
02:10:12.735 --> 02:10:15.673
Take your bag with you, because you don’t want,

1616
02:10:15.673 --> 02:10:23.184
because the church going to [pull?] around me and don’t want people to park there, and Mr. Valery going to lose some customers.”

1617
02:10:23.184 --> 02:10:30.041
She said, “You got to look out for your people in your neighborhood, because Mr. Valery look out for you all.”

1618
02:10:30.041 --> 02:10:32.000
I said, “Yeah, he do.”

1619
02:10:32.000 --> 02:10:37.286
<v ARSENEAULT>So were there any local, other local musicians that you remember?

1620
02:10:37.286 --> 02:10:42.091
<v LEE>Yeah, they had some, but not played at the Paradise.

1621
02:10:42.091 --> 02:10:45.455
<v ARSENEAULT>They just played, where did they play?

1622
02:10:45.455 --> 02:10:50.260
<v LEE>Well you had like Garfield Verdine playing with Gatemouth Brown.

1623
02:10:50.260 --> 02:10:52.182
They were big time.

1624
02:10:52.182 --> 02:10:55.065
They went all overseas and everything.

1625
02:10:55.065 --> 02:10:58.429
And Arthur [Proxide?], Garfield played with him.

1626
02:10:58.429 --> 02:11:09.961
And they had Moses O’Neil, he played the trumpet, and he played with some of the big bands up at Chicago, in that area.

1627
02:11:09.961 --> 02:11:21.974
Those were two of the best that came through around high school, around one time with us, that went on and made it big time.

1628
02:11:21.974 --> 02:11:28.701
Garfield was really, he was playing on TV when he was in high school.

1629
02:11:28.701 --> 02:11:31.104
He was talented, very talented.

1630
02:11:31.104 --> 02:11:34.948
He could play any instrument in the band.

1631
02:11:34.948 --> 02:11:46.000
And he taught a lot of, he taught me how to play the saxophone when we was in school, in junior high.

1632
02:11:46.000 --> 02:11:48.658
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you in the band?

1633
02:11:48.658 --> 02:11:59.291
<v LEE>Yeah, I played in the band until I got in about tenth, eleventh grade. [ . . . ?] Because at halftime at Mossville, football,

1634
02:11:59.291 --> 02:12:12.051
some of the athletes, we was a small school, [?] [eight?] that would play at halftime with the band in uniform, in your football uniform.

1635
02:12:12.051 --> 02:12:15.772
We didn’t have a lot of people.

1636
02:12:15.772 --> 02:12:17.899
So you can imagine.

1637
02:12:17.899 --> 02:12:25.342
Then they said, “If you going to play sports, you going to play sports.

1638
02:12:25.342 --> 02:12:30.658
You ain’t going to be up there in the band.”

1639
02:12:30.658 --> 02:12:35.975
At times you’ll be practicing, you’ll be on the field.

1640
02:12:35.975 --> 02:12:45.013
But Garfield—back to Garfield and Moses—Garfield made a couple of records with these people with Gatemouth Brown.

1641
02:12:45.013 --> 02:12:50.861
That’s that old blues, real blues, lowdown New Orleans type jazz.

1642
02:12:50.861 --> 02:12:52.987
<v ARSENEAULT>They were from Mossville?

1643
02:12:52.987 --> 02:12:54.051
<v LEE>Who that?

1644
02:12:54.051 --> 02:12:54.582
<v ARSENEAULT>Garfield?

1645
02:12:54.582 --> 02:12:55.114
<v LEE>Yeah.

1646
02:12:55.114 --> 02:13:01.494
Garfield Verdine was right there with Sasol building the plant [at now?].

1647
02:13:01.494 --> 02:13:04.152
And Moses O’Neil was, too.

1648
02:13:04.152 --> 02:13:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember the name of any of his albums?

1649
02:13:10.000 --> 02:13:14.704
<v LEE>No. I can’t remember the name of them.

1650
02:13:14.704 --> 02:13:21.171
But I know he’s on several of them with Gatemouth Brown.

1651
02:13:21.171 --> 02:13:21.759
<v ARSENEAULT>Verdine?

1652
02:13:21.759 --> 02:13:22.935
It’s V-E-R- . . .

1653
02:13:22.935 --> 02:13:25.874
<v LEE>V-E-R-D-I-N-E, I’d say, I think.

1654
02:13:25.874 --> 02:13:28.814
And Moses was an O’Neil.

1655
02:13:28.814 --> 02:13:31.754
He was a trumpet man.

1656
02:13:31.754 --> 02:13:35.869
And they both was real exceptionally good.

1657
02:13:35.869 --> 02:13:47.040
They used to have, like on the talents . . . on TV in the afternoon before the news would come on,

1658
02:13:47.040 --> 02:13:49.980
they’d have some special guests.

1659
02:13:49.980 --> 02:13:53.508
Garfield played on there several times.

1660
02:13:53.508 --> 02:13:55.271
He was unbelievable.

1661
02:13:55.271 --> 02:13:58.211
He was a one-man band.

1662
02:13:58.211 --> 02:13:58.799
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1663
02:13:58.799 --> 02:13:59.387
<v LEE>Yeah.

1664
02:13:59.387 --> 02:14:08.206
Sometimes the band instructor wouldn’t show up, because we didn’t have a fulltime band director.

1665
02:14:08.206 --> 02:14:17.613
And the days that I [?] like he had two of those schools he was working at,

1666
02:14:17.613 --> 02:14:25.256
or he’d be off sick or something, well Garfield would teach the class.

1667
02:14:25.256 --> 02:14:28.196
Because he was the man.

1668
02:14:28.196 --> 02:14:29.960
He could play.

1669
02:14:29.960 --> 02:14:32.899
He could play any instrument.

1670
02:14:32.899 --> 02:14:37.015
He taught me how to read music.

1671
02:14:37.015 --> 02:14:42.307
He taught me how to play the alto sax.

1672
02:14:42.307 --> 02:14:51.714
And I played it for about four years: junior high, and two years in high school.

1673
02:14:51.714 --> 02:14:56.417
I wish I’d have kept playing that, though.

1674
02:14:56.417 --> 02:14:57.005
Really.

1675
02:14:57.005 --> 02:15:02.296
That’s something I could do now, sit around, play.

1676
02:15:02.296 --> 02:15:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You could always pick it up again.

1677
02:15:07.000 --> 02:15:08.463
<v LEE>Yeah, but my lungs.

1678
02:15:08.463 --> 02:15:08.829
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

1679
02:15:08.829 --> 02:15:10.659
<v LEE>I can’t blow a bubble.

1680
02:15:10.659 --> 02:15:11.390
But anyway . . .

1681
02:15:11.390 --> 02:15:13.951
<v ARSENEAULT>Well, I guess that’s a good place.

1682
02:15:13.951 --> 02:15:17.244
We’re taking up a lot of your time today.

1683
02:15:17.244 --> 02:15:20.537
So thank you for your time today, Mr. Lee.

1684
02:15:20.537 --> 02:15:21.268
<v LEE>Anytime, man.

1685
02:15:21.268 --> 02:15:26.390
<v ARSENEAULT>We’ll probably set up another one, do one more, and then call it good.

1686
02:15:26.390 --> 02:15:26.756
<v LEE>Yeah.

1687
02:15:26.756 --> 02:15:27.122
Okay.

1688
02:15:27.122 --> 02:15:34.805
<v ARSENEAULT>And I have the copy of the last one for you, too, if you all want to give it a listen.

1689
02:15:34.805 --> 02:15:36.268
It’s on a CD.

1690
02:15:36.268 --> 02:15:37.000
<v LEE>Okay.

1691
02:15:37.000 --> 02:15:37.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4536.

1692
02:15:37.429 --> 02:15:37.857
End Session II.]
